window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-ncert11-phy-thermodynamics","kind":"practice","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"title":"Thermodynamics · Practice","chapter":"Thermodynamics","subject":"Physics","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-001","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":1,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A bullet moving at very high speed is not at a higher temperature merely because of its speed.\nReason (R): Internal energy of a system does not include the kinetic energy of the system as a whole; it is measured in the frame in which the centre of mass is at rest.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Temperature is linked to the disordered (random) motion of the molecules, not to the motion of the body as a whole.\nR is true. NCERT defines internal energy in the centre-of-mass frame, so the bulk kinetic energy is excluded.\nR explains A: the bullet's speed adds only bulk kinetic energy, not internal energy, so its temperature is unchanged.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-002","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":2,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"One mole of an ideal gas goes through the cycle A→B→C→A:\n- A→B: isochoric heating from $T_A = 300$ K to $T_B = 600$ K.\n- B→C: isothermal expansion at 600 K until its pressure returns to $P_A$.\n- C→A: isobaric compression back to A.\nWhat is the net heat absorbed by the gas per cycle? (R = 8.3 J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$, $\\ln 2 = 0.693$)","image":"","options":["962 J","3452 J","5942 J","2490 J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A→B: $W = 0$, and since V is fixed, $P_B = 2P_A$.\nB→C: isothermal at 600 K with P halved, so V doubles. Then $W = R(600)\\ln 2 = 3452$ J.\nC→A: $W = R(T_A - T_C) = 8.3 \\times (300 - 600) = -2490$ J.\nCycle: $\\Delta U = 0$, so net Q = net W = 3452 − 2490 ≈ 962 J.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-003","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":3,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) For an ideal gas, $C_p - C_v = R$.\n(ii) For an ideal gas, $\\gamma = \\frac{C_p}{C_v}$ is always greater than 1.\n(iii) The heat needed to raise the temperature of a given ideal gas sample by $\\Delta T$ is the same whether the volume or the pressure is held constant.\n(iv) For a solid, $C_p$ and $C_v$ are nearly equal because its volume change on heating is negligible.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: this is Mayer's relation.\n(ii) Correct: since $C_p = C_v + R$, $\\gamma > 1$.\n(iii) Incorrect: heating at constant pressure needs extra heat $\\mu R\\Delta T$ for the work done.\n(iv) Correct: for a solid $P\\Delta V$ is negligible, so $\\Delta Q \\approx \\Delta U$ in both cases.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-004","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":4,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"A Carnot engine is used to define a thermodynamic temperature. Its sink is a system at the triple point of water (273.16 K). In each cycle the engine absorbs 600 J from the source and rejects 400 J to the sink. What is the thermodynamic temperature of the source?","image":"","options":["409.7 K","182.1 K","819.5 K","546.3 K"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $Q_1$ = 600 J, $Q_2$ = 400 J, $T_2$ = 273.16 K.\nFormula: for any Carnot cycle, $\\frac{Q_1}{Q_2} = \\frac{T_1}{T_2}$. This holds whatever the working substance, so it can define a universal temperature scale.\nSubstituting: $T_1 = 273.16 \\times \\frac{600}{400}$.\nSo $T_1 \\approx$ 409.7 K.\nUsing the work done (200 J) in place of $Q_2$ would wrongly give 819.5 K.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-005","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":5,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Thermodynamics does not go into the molecular constitution of matter.\n(ii) The laws of thermodynamics were formulated before the molecular picture of matter was firmly established.\n(iii) Kinetic theory of gases involves the molecular distribution of velocities.\n(iv) Thermodynamics is mainly concerned with the motion of the system as a whole.\n(v) A microscopic description of a gas would require the coordinates and velocities of all its molecules.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: thermodynamics is a macroscopic science dealing with bulk systems.\n(ii) Correct: its concepts and laws were formulated in the nineteenth century, before the molecular picture was firmly established.\n(iii) Correct: kinetic theory is less detailed than a full microscopic description but uses velocity distributions.\n(iv) Incorrect: thermodynamics deals with the internal macroscopic state; motion of the whole body belongs to mechanics.\n(v) Correct.\nSo 4 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-006","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":6,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"A gas in a cylinder absorbs 1200 J of heat. It expands quasi-statically at a constant pressure of $2 \\times 10^5$ Pa from 1.5 L to 4.0 L. What is the change in its internal energy?","image":"","options":["700 J","1700 J","500 J","1200 J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: ΔQ = 1200 J, P = $2\\times10^5$ Pa, ΔV = (4.0 − 1.5) L = $2.5\\times10^{-3}$ m$^3$.\nFormula: ΔQ = ΔU + PΔV.\nWork done by the gas: $W = 2\\times10^5 \\times 2.5\\times10^{-3}$ = 500 J.\nSubstituting: ΔU = 1200 − 500.\nSo ΔU = 700 J. The value 1700 J wrongly adds the work instead of subtracting it.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-007","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":7,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Internal energy\n(B) Heat\n(C) Work\n(D) Kinetic energy of a gas-filled box moving as a whole\nList-II\n(i) Energy in transit due to a temperature difference\n(ii) Not included in the internal energy of the gas\n(iii) Depends only on the state, not on the path\n(iv) Energy transfer by moving a piston through raising or lowering a weight","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(i), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Internal energy is a state variable, so it matches (iii).\n(B) Heat is energy in transit due to a temperature difference, so it matches (i).\n(C) Work is energy transfer by other means, such as moving a piston by raising or lowering a weight, so it matches (iv).\n(D) The bulk kinetic energy of the box is excluded from U, so it matches (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-008","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":8,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"1 mol of a diatomic ideal gas ($\\gamma = 1.4$) at 300 K is compressed quasi-statically and adiabatically to half its initial volume. Find the work done on the gas. (Take R = 8.3 J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$ and $2^{0.4} = 1.3195$)","image":"","options":["796 J","1726 J","1989 J","2785 J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $\\mu = 1$, $T_1 = 300$ K, $\\frac{V_1}{V_2} = 2$, $\\gamma = 1.4$.\nStep 1: $T_2 = T_1\\left(\\frac{V_1}{V_2}\\right)^{\\gamma-1} = 300 \\times 1.3195 = 395.85$ K.\nStep 2: Work done on the gas $= \\frac{\\mu R(T_2 - T_1)}{\\gamma - 1} = \\frac{8.3 \\times 95.85}{0.4}$.\nSo the work done on the gas is about 1989 J. The value 1726 J is the isothermal result, $RT\\ln 2$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-009","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":9,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Adiabatic wall\n(B) Diathermic wall\n(C) Thermodynamic equilibrium\n(D) Mechanical equilibrium\nList-II\n(I) Net external force and torque on the system are zero\n(II) Macroscopic variables of the system do not change with time\n(III) Insulating wall that does not allow heat flow\n(IV) Conducting wall that allows heat flow","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) An adiabatic wall is insulating and blocks heat flow, so A matches III.\n(B) A diathermic wall is conducting and lets heat flow, so B matches IV.\n(C) Thermodynamic equilibrium means the macroscopic variables do not change in time, so C matches II.\n(D) Mechanical equilibrium means zero net external force and torque, so D matches I.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-010","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":10,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"A book lying on a table never jumps up by itself, with the table cooling and supplying the book's mechanical energy. Which principle rules out this process?","image":"","options":["First law of thermodynamics","Second law of thermodynamics","Zeroth law of thermodynamics","Law of conservation of energy"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The table could, in principle, give up internal energy equal to the book's gain in potential energy, so energy would be conserved.\nThe first law (energy conservation) therefore does not forbid this process.\nThe zeroth law only defines temperature, so it says nothing about this process either.\nThe process is still never observed. The extra principle that forbids processes the first law allows is the second law.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-011","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":11,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are extensive quantities?\nInternal energy, pressure, volume, density, temperature, total mass, heat capacity $S = \\frac{\\Delta Q}{\\Delta T}$, specific heat capacity $s$","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Halve the system and check which quantities are halved.\nExtensive: internal energy, volume, total mass, and heat capacity S (it is proportional to mass).\nIntensive: pressure, temperature, density, and specific heat capacity s (it is S per unit mass, so it does not depend on amount).\nThat gives 4 extensive quantities.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-012","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":12,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"A metallic element obeys the equipartition prediction for the molar specific heat of solids at room temperature. Its measured specific heat capacity is 390 J kg$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$. What is the approximate molar mass of the element? (R = 8.31 J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["21 g/mol","39 g/mol","64 g/mol","128 g/mol"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: s = 390 J kg$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$, and C = 3R = 24.93 J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$.\nFormula: molar heat capacity = specific heat × molar mass, so C = sM and $M = \\frac{3R}{s}$.\nSubstituting: $M = \\frac{24.93}{390}$ = 0.0639 kg/mol.\nSo M ≈ 64 g/mol (close to copper). The value 21 g/mol results from using R instead of 3R.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-013","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":13,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following processes are irreversible?\n(i) Heat flowing from the hot base of a vessel, just taken off an oven, to its cooler parts\n(ii) Free expansion of a gas into vacuum\n(iii) Cooking gas leaking from a cylinder and spreading through the kitchen\n(iv) Quasi-static isothermal expansion of an ideal gas in a cylinder with a frictionless piston\n(v) A rotating blade in a liquid coming to rest because of viscosity","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Irreversible: the cooler parts will not spontaneously cool further and reheat the base, as that would violate the second law.\n(ii) Irreversible: during free expansion the gas passes through non-equilibrium states.\n(iii) Irreversible: diffusion never reverses by itself to refill the cylinder.\n(iv) Reversible: this process is quasi-static and has no dissipative effects.\n(v) Irreversible: viscosity is a dissipative effect that turns mechanical energy into internal energy of the liquid.\nSo 4 of the 5 processes are irreversible.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-014","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":14,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"An irreversible engine I and a reversible engine R work between the same source and sink. I absorbs 1000 J from the source and is claimed to deliver 400 J of work. R runs as a refrigerator: it returns 1000 J to the source, takes 700 J from the sink and needs 300 J of work. The two are coupled so that R's work comes from I's output. What is the net effect of the combined system per cycle?","image":"","options":["No net effect anywhere, so the claim is consistent with the second law","100 J of heat is transferred from the sink to the source with no work, violating the Clausius statement","100 J is extracted from the sink and converted entirely into work, with no change in the source, violating the Kelvin-Planck statement","100 J of work is converted into heat in the sink, which is allowed"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Source: it loses 1000 J to I and regains 1000 J from R, so its net change is zero.\nSink: it gains $1000 - 400 = 600$ J from I and loses 700 J to R, a net loss of 100 J.\nWork: I produces 400 J and R uses 300 J, leaving a net output of 100 J.\nThe sole result is 100 J taken from one reservoir and turned fully into work. This violates Kelvin-Planck, so I cannot be more efficient than R.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-015","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":15,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Two Carnot engines work between the same pair of reservoirs. One uses helium as its working substance and the other uses a real vapour. According to Carnot's theorem, their efficiencies are","image":"","options":["higher for helium, because it behaves more like an ideal gas","higher for the vapour, because it has larger heat capacity","equal, because Carnot efficiency does not depend on the working substance","equal only if both substances have the same value of $\\gamma$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Carnot's theorem (part b) says the efficiency of a Carnot engine does not depend on the nature of the working substance.\nIt depends only on the reservoir temperatures: $\\eta = 1 - \\frac{T_2}{T_1}$.\nAn ideal gas is used in the derivation only because its equation of state is simple; the result holds for any substance.\n$\\gamma$ cancels out of the final efficiency expression.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-016","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":16,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"For a fixed amount of gas, the curve of pressure against volume drawn at a constant temperature is called","image":"","options":["an isotherm","an adiabat","an isobar","an isochore"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT defines the pressure–volume curve for a fixed temperature as an isotherm. For an ideal gas it obeys $PV$ = constant.\nAn adiabat is the P-V curve when no heat flows.\nIsobars and isochores refer to constant pressure and constant volume respectively.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-017","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":17,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Using the equipartition prediction for the molar specific heat of a solid, estimate the specific heat capacity of copper at room temperature. Molar mass of copper = 63.5 g mol$^{-1}$, R = 8.31 J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$.","image":"","options":["392.6 J kg$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$","130.9 J kg$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$","261.7 J kg$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$","0.393 J kg$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: M = 63.5 g mol$^{-1}$ = 0.0635 kg mol$^{-1}$, R = 8.31 J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$.\nFormula: each atom behaves as a 3D oscillator with average energy $3k_BT$, so $C = 3R$ and $s = \\frac{C}{M}$.\nSubstituting: $C = 3 \\times 8.31 = 24.93$ J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$, so $s = \\frac{24.93}{0.0635}$.\nSo s $\\approx$ 392.6 J kg$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$. The value 0.393 comes from leaving the molar mass in grams.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-018","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":18,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following processes are irreversible?\n(i) A vessel removed from an oven reaching a uniform temperature as heat flows from its base to its other parts\n(ii) Diffusion of leaked cooking gas throughout a room\n(iii) Combustion of a petrol-air mixture ignited by a spark\n(iv) Quasi-static isothermal expansion of an ideal gas with a frictionless piston\n(v) A rotating blade in a liquid coming to rest because of viscosity\n(vi) Free expansion of a gas into vacuum","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Irreversible: the base will never spontaneously reheat while the rest of the vessel cools.\n(ii) Irreversible: the diffused gas does not return to the cylinder by itself.\n(iii) Irreversible: an explosive reaction passes through non-equilibrium states.\n(iv) Reversible: the process is quasi-static and non-dissipative.\n(v) Irreversible: viscosity is a dissipative effect.\n(vi) Irreversible: the gas passes through non-equilibrium states.\nSo 5 processes are irreversible.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-019","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":19,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"A gas in a cylinder is kept at a constant pressure of $2.0 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa. It expands slowly from 1.0 L to 4.0 L. What work is done by the gas?","image":"","options":["600 J","300 J","$6.0 \\times 10^{5}$ J","1000 J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $P = 2.0 \\times 10^5$ Pa, $V_1 = 1.0 \\times 10^{-3}$ m$^3$, $V_2 = 4.0 \\times 10^{-3}$ m$^3$.\nFormula: for an isobaric process, $W = P(V_2 - V_1)$.\nSubstituting: $W = 2.0 \\times 10^5 \\times 3.0 \\times 10^{-3}$.\nSo W = 600 J. The option $6.0 \\times 10^5$ J comes from forgetting to convert litres to m$^3$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-020","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":20,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"2 mol of an ideal gas are kept in a rigid closed vessel. Its molar specific heat at constant volume is $20.8$ J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$. How much heat must be supplied to raise its temperature by 10 K?","image":"","options":["416 J","208 J","832 J","582 J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\mu = 2$ mol, $C_v = 20.8$ J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$, $\\Delta T = 10$ K. The vessel is rigid, so the process is isochoric.\nFormula: $Q = \\mu C_v \\Delta T$ (no work is done, so all the heat raises U).\nSubstituting: $Q = 2 \\times 20.8 \\times 10$.\nSo Q = 416 J.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-021","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":21,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: If an irreversible engine I had a greater efficiency than a reversible engine R working between the same source and sink, then running R as a refrigerator driven by I would make the combined system extract a net heat $W' - W$ from the cold reservoir and convert it fully into work, violating the Kelvin-Planck statement.\nStatement II: The efficiency $1 - \\frac{T_2}{T_1}$ of a Carnot engine holds only when the working substance is an ideal gas, since its derivation uses $PV = \\mu RT$.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Suppose $\\eta_I > \\eta_R$, so W' > W for the same $Q_1$. With R running as a refrigerator, R takes $Q_1 - W$ from the sink while I returns only $Q_1 - W'$.\nThe net effect is that heat $W' - W$ is drawn from the sink and turned entirely into work. Kelvin-Planck forbids this, which is Carnot's theorem.\nStatement II is incorrect. The Carnot efficiency is independent of the working substance. The ideal gas is used only because its equation of state makes the calculation easy.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-022","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":22,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"A Carnot engine takes heat from a source at 327 °C and rejects heat to a sink at 27 °C. What is its efficiency?","image":"","options":["50%","91.7%","8.3%","25%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $T_1$ = 327 + 273 = 600 K and $T_2$ = 27 + 273 = 300 K.\nFormula: $\\eta = 1 - \\frac{T_2}{T_1}$, with both temperatures in kelvin.\nSubstituting: $\\eta = 1 - \\frac{300}{600} = 0.5$.\nIf you use Celsius values instead, you get $1 - \\frac{27}{327} \\approx$ 91.7%, which is wrong.\nSo $\\eta$ = 50%.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-023","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":23,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"In the derivation of Mayer's relation for one mole of an ideal gas, $C_p = \\left(\\frac{\\Delta U}{\\Delta T}\\right)_p + P\\left(\\frac{\\Delta V}{\\Delta T}\\right)_p$. The second term, $P\\left(\\frac{\\Delta V}{\\Delta T}\\right)_p$, is equal to:","image":"","options":["$R$","$C_v$","$\\gamma R$","Zero"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"For one mole of an ideal gas, $PV = RT$.\nAt constant pressure, a change $\\Delta T$ gives $P\\Delta V = R\\Delta T$, so $P\\left(\\frac{\\Delta V}{\\Delta T}\\right)_p = R$.\nThe first term is $C_v$, because U of an ideal gas depends only on T. This gives $C_p = C_v + R$.\nThe term is not zero, because the gas expands at constant pressure.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-024","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":24,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Match each hypothetical device or process in List-I with its correct description in List-II.\nList-I:\n(A) A device working in a cycle that absorbs heat from a single reservoir and converts all of it into work\n(B) A device whose only effect is to transfer heat from a colder body to a hotter body\n(C) A machine that delivers more work per cycle than the total energy supplied to it\n(D) Quasi-static isothermal expansion of an ideal gas with a frictionless piston\nList-II:\n(P) Violates the first law\n(Q) Violates the Kelvin-Planck statement\n(R) Violates the Clausius statement\n(S) Violates no law and is reversible","image":"","options":["A-R, B-Q, C-P, D-S","A-Q, B-R, C-P, D-S","A-Q, B-R, C-S, D-P","A-P, B-R, C-Q, D-S"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(A) A perfect heat engine is exactly what the Kelvin-Planck statement forbids, so A-Q.\n(B) A perfect refrigerator, with heat moving from cold to hot as the sole result, is forbidden by Clausius, so B-R.\n(C) Getting more work out than energy put in breaks energy conservation, so C-P.\n(D) This process is quasi-static and non-dissipative, so it is allowed and reversible, so D-S.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-025","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":25,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are intensive thermodynamic variables?\nPressure, temperature, density, internal energy, volume, total mass","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Test: divide a system in equilibrium into two equal halves.\nVariables that keep the same value in each half are intensive: pressure, temperature and density.\nVariables that get halved are extensive: internal energy, volume and total mass.\nSo there are 3 intensive variables.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-026","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":26,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: One calorie is the amount of heat needed to raise the temperature of 1 g of water from 14.5 °C to 15.5 °C.\nStatement II: The specific heat of water is exactly the same at every temperature between 0 °C and 100 °C, so the temperature interval in the definition of the calorie is not essential.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I: Correct. This is the precise NCERT definition of the calorie.\nStatement II: Incorrect. Precise measurements (Fig. 11.5) show that the specific heat of water varies slightly with temperature between 0 and 100 °C.\nBecause of this variation, the unit temperature interval (14.5 °C to 15.5 °C) has to be stated to make the definition exact.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-027","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":27,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For an ideal gas, the molar specific heat at constant pressure is greater than that at constant volume.\nReason (R): When heat is supplied at constant pressure, part of it is used by the gas to do work on the surroundings as it expands.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: $C_p - C_v = R > 0$.\nR is true: at constant pressure the gas expands and does work $P\\Delta V$. Only the remaining heat raises U.\nSo more heat is needed for the same $\\Delta T$ at constant pressure, which is exactly why $C_p > C_v$. R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-028","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":28,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"The Carnot cycle of an ideal reversible heat engine consists of","image":"","options":["two isobaric processes joined by two isochoric processes","two isothermal processes joined by two isochoric processes","two isothermal processes joined by two adiabatic processes","two adiabatic processes joined by two isobaric processes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A reversible engine using only two reservoirs must take in and give out heat isothermally, at $T_1$ and at $T_2$.\nIt must change temperature between $T_1$ and $T_2$ with no heat exchange, which means reversible adiabatic steps.\nAn isochoric step would need a whole series of reservoirs between $T_2$ and $T_1$ to stay quasi-static. A two-reservoir Carnot engine cannot have that.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-029","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":29,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about a gas in a cylinder fitted with a movable piston:\n(i) A gas in a given equilibrium state possesses a definite amount of internal energy.\n(ii) A gas in a given equilibrium state contains a definite amount of heat.\n(iii) It is meaningful to say that a certain amount of work was done by the gas while it went from state A to state B.\n(iv) The change in internal energy between two given states depends on whether the change was produced by heating the gas or by pushing the piston.\n(v) Heat is energy transferred between the system and its surroundings because of a temperature difference between them.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: internal energy $U$ is a state variable, so every state has a definite $U$.\n(ii) Incorrect: heat is energy in transit, not something a body contains. The statement is as meaningless as saying a gas 'has a certain amount of work'.\n(iii) Correct: work is a mode of energy transfer during a process, so this statement is meaningful.\n(iv) Incorrect: $\\Delta U$ depends only on the initial and final states, not on the path or the mode of transfer.\n(v) Correct: this is how heat is defined. Work is energy transfer by other means.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-030","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":30,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"In 1798 Count Rumford observed the boring of a brass cannon. Which observation from this experiment went against the caloric (heat-fluid) picture of heat?","image":"","options":["A sharper drill produced noticeably more heat than a blunt one","The heat produced depended on the work done by the horses turning the drill, not on how sharp the drill was","Heat was produced only while the cannon was already hotter than its surroundings","The heat produced depended only on the mass of brass removed from the cannon"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Caloric theory treated heat as a fluid stored in the pores of a substance, so a sharper drill should scoop out more of it.\nRumford found that the heat produced depended on the work done by the horses turning the drill. It did not depend on the sharpness of the drill.\nThe heat was enough to boil water. This showed that work was being converted into heat, so heat is a form of energy.\nOption (1) is what caloric theory predicted, but it was not observed.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-031","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":31,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Four systems P, Q, R and S are tested in pairs through diathermic walls, one pair at a time. It is found that P is in thermal equilibrium with Q, and Q is in thermal equilibrium with R. However, when S is brought in contact with R, the state of S changes. Which conclusion is correct?","image":"","options":["P and R are in thermal equilibrium, but S is not in thermal equilibrium with P","P and R are not necessarily in thermal equilibrium, since they were never placed in contact","S is in thermal equilibrium with P because P was never placed in contact with S","P and R are in thermal equilibrium only if their pressures are equal"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"By the Zeroth Law, $T_P = T_Q$ and $T_Q = T_R$, so $T_P = T_R$. P and R are in thermal equilibrium even without direct contact, so (2) is wrong.\nS changes state on contact with R, so $T_S \\neq T_R = T_P$. Hence S is not in equilibrium with P, which rules out (3).\nThermal equilibrium needs equal temperatures, not equal pressures, so (4) is wrong.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-032","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":32,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"A sealed box containing an ideal gas is at rest on the floor of a train. The train then moves with a constant high velocity, and the gas temperature stays the same. Which statement about the internal energy $U$ of the gas is correct?","image":"","options":["$U$ increases by the kinetic energy of the box moving as a whole.","$U$ is unchanged, because the kinetic energy of the bulk motion of the box is not part of $U$.","$U$ increases because the gas molecules now move faster relative to the ground.","$U$ decreases because part of the molecular kinetic energy is converted into bulk motion."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Internal energy is the sum of the molecular kinetic and potential energies. These are measured in the frame in which the centre of mass of the system is at rest.\nIt includes only the disordered (random) motion of the molecules. The kinetic energy of the system moving as a whole is excluded.\nThe temperature, and so the random molecular motion, is unchanged, so $U$ is unchanged.\nOption (3) is the tempting one. It uses ground-frame speeds, but the molecular speed added by the train's motion is ordered bulk motion, not internal energy. This is the same reason a fast bullet is not hotter.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-033","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":33,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about quasi-static processes of an ideal gas:\n(i) In an isochoric process, no work is done by or on the gas.\n(ii) In an isobaric heating, all the heat supplied goes into increasing the internal energy.\n(iii) In a cyclic process, the change in internal energy over one complete cycle is zero.\n(iv) In an isothermal compression, the gas gives out heat.\n(v) The work done by the gas between two given states is the same for every path.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $\\Delta V = 0$, so W = 0.\n(ii) Incorrect: at constant pressure, part of the heat does work $P\\Delta V$.\n(iii) Correct: U is a state variable, and the system returns to its initial state.\n(iv) Correct: $\\Delta U = 0$ and W < 0, so Q < 0.\n(v) Incorrect: W, like Q, depends on the path; only $\\Delta U$ (equal to $\\Delta Q - \\Delta W$) is path-independent.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-034","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":34,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"A lead bullet of mass 10 g moving at 200 m/s gets embedded in a wooden block and stops. Half of its kinetic energy is converted into internal energy of the bullet. The specific heat capacity of lead is 128 J kg$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$. What is the rise in temperature of the bullet?","image":"","options":["78.1 K","156.3 K","39.1 K","7.8 K"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: m = 0.01 kg, v = 200 m/s, s = 128 J kg$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$, and half of the kinetic energy is absorbed by the bullet.\nFormula: $\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}mv^2\\right) = ms\\Delta T$\nSubstituting: the kinetic energy is $\\frac{1}{2}(0.01)(200)^2 = 200$ J, so the bullet absorbs 100 J.\nThen $\\Delta T = \\frac{100}{0.01 \\times 128} = 78.1$ K.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-035","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":35,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (process of an ideal gas) with List-II (feature):\nList-I: (A) Isothermal (B) Adiabatic (C) Isochoric (D) Isobaric\nList-II: (p) Work done by gas = $\\mu R(T_2 - T_1)$ (q) Heat absorbed goes entirely into internal energy (r) Work done by gas equals decrease in internal energy (s) Heat absorbed equals work done by gas","image":"","options":["A-s, B-r, C-q, D-p","A-r, B-s, C-q, D-p","A-s, B-r, C-p, D-q","A-q, B-r, C-s, D-p"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Isothermal: for an ideal gas $\\Delta U = 0$, so Q = W. This gives A-s.\nAdiabatic: Q = 0, so W = −$\\Delta U$. This gives B-r.\nIsochoric: no volume change, so W = 0 and Q = $\\Delta U$. This gives C-q.\nIsobaric: $W = P(V_2-V_1) = \\mu R(T_2-T_1)$. This gives D-p.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-036","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":36,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Two moles of an ideal gas with $\\gamma = 1.4$ expand adiabatically. During the expansion the temperature falls from 400 K to 300 K. How much work is done by the gas? (Take $R = 8.3$ J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["4150 J","1660 J","2490 J","5810 J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\mu = 2$, $T_1 = 400$ K, $T_2 = 300$ K, $\\gamma = 1.4$.\nFormula: For an adiabatic change, $W = \\frac{\\mu R(T_1 - T_2)}{\\gamma - 1}$.\nSubstituting: $W = \\frac{2 \\times 8.3 \\times 100}{0.4}$.\nThe value 1660 J is only $\\mu R\\Delta T$; it leaves out the factor $\\frac{1}{\\gamma-1}$.\nSo W = 4150 J. This work comes entirely from the internal energy, which is why the gas cools.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-037","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":37,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"A gas is taken from state D ($P = 3.0 \\times 10^5$ Pa, $V = 2.0$ L) to state E ($P = 1.0 \\times 10^5$ Pa, $V = 6.0$ L). On the P-V diagram, this path is a straight line. The gas is then compressed isobarically from E to state F, where its volume is back to 2.0 L. What is the total work done by the gas from D to E to F?","image":"","options":["400 J","800 J","1200 J","$-400$ J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"D→E is linear, so the work equals the trapezium area under the line: $W_{DE} = \\frac{1}{2}(3.0 + 1.0) \\times 10^5 \\times 4.0 \\times 10^{-3} = 800$ J.\nE→F is an isobaric compression at $1.0 \\times 10^5$ Pa: $W_{EF} = 1.0 \\times 10^5 \\times (2.0 - 6.0) \\times 10^{-3} = -400$ J.\nTotal: W = 800 − 400 = 400 J. This equals the area of triangle DEF.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-038","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":38,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The Zeroth Law of Thermodynamics was formulated by R.H. Fowler in 1931, after the first and second laws had already been stated and numbered.\nStatement II: The Zeroth Law of Thermodynamics leads directly to the concept of internal energy as the quantity that is equal for two systems in thermal equilibrium.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. R.H. Fowler stated the Zeroth Law in 1931, long after the first and second laws had been named.\nStatement II is incorrect. The variable that has the same value for two systems in thermal equilibrium is temperature, not internal energy.\nTwo systems in equilibrium can have very different internal energies, for example because they have different masses.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-039","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":39,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Free expansion of a gas into vacuum is an irreversible process","A quasi-static isothermal expansion of an ideal gas in a cylinder with a frictionless piston is reversible","Any quasi-static process is reversible, even if friction acts between the piston and the cylinder","Irreversibility arises mainly from passage through non-equilibrium states and from dissipative effects such as friction and viscosity"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct. During free expansion the gas passes through non-equilibrium states, and the process cannot be undone.\n(2) Correct. NCERT gives this as its standard example of a reversible process.\n(3) Incorrect. Reversibility needs two conditions: the process must be quasi-static AND have no dissipative effects. Friction makes the process irreversible even when it is slow.\n(4) Correct. These are the two causes of irreversibility listed in NCERT.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-040","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":40,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"In 1798, Count Rumford observed the boring of a brass cannon. Which observation most strongly went against the caloric theory of heat?","image":"","options":["The heat produced was enough to boil water","The heat produced depended on the work done by the horses and not on the sharpness of the drill","A sharper drill produced much more heat than a blunt drill","The brass cannon became colder after boring"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In the caloric picture, heat is a fluid stored in the pores of a substance, so a sharper drill should scoop out more of it.\nRumford found that the heat produced depended only on the work done by the horses turning the drill, not on how sharp the drill was.\nThe best explanation is that work is converted into heat, so heat is a form of energy.\nOption (1) is true, but a large amount of heat alone does not contradict the caloric idea.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-041","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":41,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"In each cycle, a heat engine absorbs 2400 J of heat from a hot reservoir and rejects 1800 J to a cold reservoir. What is its efficiency?","image":"","options":["75%","25%","33.3%","133%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $Q_1 = 2400$ J and $Q_2 = 1800$ J.\nFormula: $\\eta = \\frac{W}{Q_1} = 1 - \\frac{Q_2}{Q_1}$, with $W = Q_1 - Q_2 = 600$ J.\nSubstituting: $\\eta = \\frac{600}{2400} = 0.25$.\nSo $\\eta = 25\\%$. The engine rejects some heat, so $\\eta < 1$, as the second law requires.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-042","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":42,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"When 1 g of water at atmospheric pressure ($1.013 \\times 10^5$ Pa) changes from liquid (volume 1 cm$^3$) to vapour (volume 1671 cm$^3$), it absorbs its latent heat of 2256 J. Consider the following statements:\n(i) The work done by the water on the surroundings is about 169 J.\n(ii) The internal energy of the water increases by about 2087 J.\n(iii) Since the temperature stays constant during boiling, $\\Delta U = 0$ and all 2256 J appears as work.\n(iv) Most of the heat absorbed goes into increasing the internal energy rather than into work.\nHow many of these statements are correct?","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct. $W = P\\Delta V \\approx 1.013\\times10^5 \\times 1670\\times10^{-6} \\approx 169$ J. (NCERT uses 1671 cm$^3$ and gets 169.2 J.)\n(ii) Correct. $\\Delta U = 2256 - 169.2 = 2086.8$ J.\n(iii) Incorrect. The rule '$\\Delta U = 0$ at constant T' holds only for an ideal gas. During a phase change, intermolecular potential energy rises sharply even at constant temperature.\n(iv) Correct. About 2087 J of the 2256 J goes into internal energy.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-043","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":43,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In a cyclic process, the net heat absorbed by the system equals the net work done by the system.\nStatement II: In a cyclic process, the net work done by the system is always zero because the system returns to its initial state.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: U is a state variable, so $\\Delta U = 0$ over a cycle. The first law then gives $Q = W$.\nStatement II is incorrect: work is not a state variable. Net work equals the area enclosed by the cycle on the P-V diagram, which is generally non-zero.\nOnly $\\Delta U$ (not W) vanishes when the system returns to its initial state.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-044","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":44,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In deriving $C_p - C_v = R$ for an ideal gas, $\\left(\\frac{\\Delta U}{\\Delta T}\\right)_p$ can be replaced by $C_v$ because the internal energy of an ideal gas depends only on its temperature.\nStatement II: Since the relation follows from the first law alone, $C_p - C_v = R$ holds exactly for every real gas, liquid and solid.","image":"","options":["Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both statements are correct","Both statements are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: U of an ideal gas is a function of T only. So $\\frac{\\Delta U}{\\Delta T}$ is the same at constant P or constant V, and equals $C_v$.\nStatement II is incorrect: the derivation also uses $PV = RT$, which gives $P\\Delta V = R\\Delta T$, and U = U(T). Both are ideal-gas properties.\nFor solids $\\Delta V$ is negligible, so $C_p \\approx C_v$, not $C_v + R$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-045","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":45,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"A book resting on a table never jumps up on its own by drawing energy from the table, which would cool down slightly. If it did, its gain in potential energy would exactly equal the loss in internal energy of the table. Which law rules out this event?","image":"","options":["Zeroth law of thermodynamics","First law of thermodynamics","Second law of thermodynamics","Law of conservation of momentum"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The proposed event conserves energy. The table's loss of internal energy equals the book's gain in mechanical energy, so the first law is satisfied.\nEven so, such an event is never observed. NCERT uses this example to show that some extra principle forbids processes that the first law allows.\nThat principle is the second law of thermodynamics. Turning disordered internal energy entirely into ordered mechanical energy as the sole result is not possible.\nThe zeroth law deals only with thermal equilibrium and temperature. It says nothing about this event.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-046","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":46,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In a quasi-static isothermal expansion, an ideal gas absorbs heat from the reservoir even though at every stage its temperature equals that of the reservoir.\nReason (R): In a quasi-static process, the temperature of the system and that of the reservoir differ by a finite amount at every stage.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. In isothermal expansion, $\\Delta U = 0$, so Q = W > 0 and the gas absorbs heat.\nR is false. In a quasi-static process the temperature difference is only infinitesimal. A finite difference would drive the gas through non-equilibrium states.\nThe heat flow is made possible by this infinitesimal difference, which is not what R states.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-047","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":47,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"14 g of nitrogen gas (molar mass 28 g/mol, treated as an ideal diatomic gas with $C_p = \\frac{7}{2}R$) is heated at constant pressure. Its temperature rises by 20 K. How much heat is supplied? (Take $R = 8.3$ J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$.)","image":"","options":["290.5 J","207.5 J","581 J","83 J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: m = 14 g, M = 28 g/mol, so $\\mu = 0.5$ mol. Also $C_p = 3.5R$ and $\\Delta T = 20$ K.\nFormula: $Q = \\mu C_p \\Delta T$.\nSubstituting: $Q = 0.5 \\times 3.5 \\times 8.3 \\times 20$.\nSo Q = 290.5 J.\nUsing $C_v$ instead gives 207.5 J, which is wrong because the pressure is constant.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-048","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":48,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Two gases A and B in separate containers are separated by a wall. Their macroscopic variables change on their own until both reach new equilibrium states, after which nothing changes. The wall separating them must be","image":"","options":["an adiabatic wall, which allows heat to flow","a diathermic wall, which does not allow heat to flow","a diathermic wall, which allows heat to flow","an adiabatic wall, which does not allow heat to flow"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A diathermic wall is a conducting wall that allows energy (heat) to flow between the systems.\nAn adiabatic wall is an insulating wall that blocks heat flow.\nSpontaneous change of the variables until thermal equilibrium is reached needs heat flow, so the wall is diathermic and conducting.\nOptions (1) and (2) mix up the two definitions. Option (4) would give no change at all.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-049","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":49,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For an ideal gas, the molar specific heat at constant pressure is greater than that at constant volume, with $C_p - C_v = R$.\nReason (R): At constant pressure, part of the heat supplied is used in doing work $P\\Delta V$ during expansion, and the internal energy of an ideal gas depends only on its temperature.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. This is the standard result $C_p - C_v = R$ for an ideal gas.\nR is true. At constant volume, all the heat raises U, so $C_v = \\frac{\\Delta U}{\\Delta T}$.\nAt constant pressure, the same $\\Delta U$ occurs for the same $\\Delta T$, because U depends only on T. Extra heat is also needed for the work $P\\Delta V$.\nFor one mole, $P\\frac{\\Delta V}{\\Delta T} = R$, which gives $C_p = C_v + R$. So R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-050","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":50,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"In which of the following situations can the gas NOT be described by thermodynamic state variables such as a single pressure and temperature?","image":"","options":["A gas in a closed, rigid, insulated container whose P, V and T do not change with time","A gas rushing into an evacuated region just after a partition is suddenly removed","A gas that has settled to uniform temperature and pressure after being heated","An ideal gas expanding quasi-statically at constant temperature"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"State variables describe only equilibrium states.\nIn free expansion, the gas passes through non-equilibrium states during the rapid expansion. Its pressure is not uniform, so no single P or T exists.\nOptions (1) and (3) are equilibrium states.\nIn a quasi-static process, the gas is in equilibrium at every stage.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-051","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":51,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"A Carnot engine works between a source at 527 °C and a sink at 127 °C. What is its efficiency?","image":"","options":["25%","50%","75.9%","24.1%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $T_1 = 527 + 273 = 800$ K and $T_2 = 127 + 273 = 400$ K.\nFormula: $\\eta = 1 - \\frac{T_2}{T_1}$, with temperatures in kelvin.\nSubstituting: $\\eta = 1 - \\frac{400}{800} = 0.5$.\nSo $\\eta$ = 50%.\nUsing Celsius values would wrongly give $1 - \\frac{127}{527} = 75.9\\%$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-052","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":52,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"A monatomic ideal gas ($\\gamma = \\frac{5}{3}$) at 300 K is compressed quasi-statically and adiabatically to one-eighth of its initial volume. What is its final temperature?","image":"","options":["600 K","1200 K","2400 K","9600 K"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $T_1 = 300$ K, $\\frac{V_1}{V_2} = 8$, $\\gamma - 1 = \\frac{2}{3}$.\nFormula: $T_1V_1^{\\gamma-1} = T_2V_2^{\\gamma-1}$, so $T_2 = T_1\\left(\\frac{V_1}{V_2}\\right)^{\\gamma-1}$.\nSubstituting: $T_2 = 300 \\times 8^{2/3} = 300 \\times 4$.\nSo $T_2$ = 1200 K. The gas heats up on adiabatic compression.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-053","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":53,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"A Carnot engine works between a source at 500 K and a sink at 300 K. In each cycle it absorbs 1200 J of heat from the source. How much work does it deliver per cycle?","image":"","options":["480 J","720 J","400 J","600 J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $T_1$ = 500 K, $T_2$ = 300 K, $Q_1$ = 1200 J.\nFormula: $\\eta = 1-\\frac{T_2}{T_1}$ and $W = \\eta Q_1$.\nSubstituting: $\\eta = 1-\\frac{300}{500} = 0.4$, so $W = 0.4 \\times 1200$.\nSo W = 480 J. The value 720 J is the heat $Q_2$ rejected to the sink, not the work.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-054","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":54,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"An ideal gas in a thermally insulated cylinder A is allowed to expand freely into an identical evacuated insulated cylinder B by suddenly opening a stopcock. Consider the following statements:\n(i) The heat exchanged with the surroundings is zero.\n(ii) The work done by the gas is zero.\n(iii) The internal energy of the gas does not change.\n(iv) The intermediate states of the gas lie on its P-V-T surface.\n(v) The final pressure is half the initial pressure.\nHow many of these statements are correct?","image":"","options":["5","4","3","2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the system is insulated.\n(ii) Correct: the gas expands against vacuum, so there is no external pressure to work against.\n(iii) Correct: $\\Delta U = \\Delta Q - \\Delta W = 0$.\n(iv) Incorrect: free expansion passes through non-equilibrium states, which have no well-defined P and T.\n(v) Correct: T is unchanged and the volume doubles, so $P_f = \\frac{P_i}{2}$.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-055","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":55,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"In thermodynamics, heat is defined as energy transfer between a system and its surroundings that occurs because of:","image":"","options":["A difference in pressure between them","A difference in temperature between them","Movement of the piston of the cylinder","A difference in internal energy between them"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Heat is energy transfer caused by a temperature difference between the system and its surroundings.\nWork is energy transfer brought about by other means, such as moving a piston by raising or lowering a weight. So (3) describes work, not heat.\nThe direction of heat flow is set by temperature, not by internal energy. A large, cooler body can have more internal energy than a small hot body, so (4) fails.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-056","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":56,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"An ideal gas is heated in a rigid closed container, so its volume stays fixed. How many of the following statements are correct for this process?\n(i) The work done by the gas is zero.\n(ii) The heat absorbed equals the increase in internal energy.\n(iii) The temperature of the gas stays constant.\n(iv) The rise in temperature for a given amount of heat is set by the specific heat at constant volume.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $\\Delta V = 0$, so $W = P\\Delta V = 0$.\n(ii) Correct: the first law gives $Q = \\Delta U + 0 = \\Delta U$.\n(iii) Incorrect: the heat raises the internal energy, so the temperature rises.\n(iv) Correct: in an isochoric process, $C_v$ fixes the temperature change.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-057","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":57,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"An electric heater supplies heat to a gas at a steady rate of 250 W. At the same time, the gas pushes a piston and does work on the surroundings at a rate of 180 J/s. At what rate does the internal energy of the gas increase?","image":"","options":["430 W","70 W","180 W","250 W"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: heat supplied per second $\\frac{\\Delta Q}{\\Delta t}$ = 250 J/s and work done by the gas per second $\\frac{\\Delta W}{\\Delta t}$ = 180 J/s.\nFormula: First law, $\\Delta U = \\Delta Q - \\Delta W$, applied to each second.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{\\Delta U}{\\Delta t}$ = 250 − 180 = 70 J/s.\nSo the internal energy rises at 70 W. Adding the two rates (430 W) is wrong, because the work done by the gas is energy leaving the system.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-058","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":58,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following processes can be treated as reversible?","image":"","options":["Free expansion of a gas into vacuum","Cooking gas leaking from a cylinder and spreading through a kitchen","Stirring a liquid that is in thermal contact with a reservoir","Quasi-static isothermal expansion of an ideal gas in a cylinder with a frictionless piston"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A process is reversible only if it is quasi-static and free of dissipative effects.\nFree expansion passes through non-equilibrium states, so it is irreversible.\nDiffusion of gas is a spontaneous process and does not reverse by itself.\nStirring dissipates work as heat, so it cannot be reversed exactly.\nA quasi-static isothermal expansion with a frictionless piston meets both conditions. NCERT gives it as an example of a reversible process.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-059","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":59,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements about a gas in a given equilibrium state is physically meaningful?","image":"","options":["The gas has a certain amount of heat.","The gas has a certain amount of work.","The gas has a certain amount of internal energy.","The gas has stored a definite amount of heat and work in it."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The state of a system is characterised by its internal energy, which is a state variable.\nHeat and work are energy in transit, that is, modes of energy transfer. A system does not 'possess' them.\nSo saying a gas 'has a certain amount of heat' or 'has a certain amount of work' is meaningless.\nSaying it has a certain amount of internal energy is meaningful.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-060","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":60,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): No engine working between a source at $T_1$ and a sink at $T_2$ can have an efficiency greater than that of a Carnot engine working between the same reservoirs.\nReason (R): The efficiency of a Carnot engine is independent of the nature of the working substance.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true (Carnot's theorem). Suppose an irreversible engine I were more efficient and drove a Carnot refrigerator R. The coupled system would then extract heat $W'-W$ from the sink and convert it fully into work. This violates the Kelvin-Planck statement.\nR is also true and is the second part of Carnot's theorem.\nHowever, A follows from the second-law argument, not from substance-independence. So R does not explain A.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-061","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":61,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"An ideal gas expands adiabatically and quasi-statically. Which of the following statements about this process is correct?","image":"","options":["The temperature of the gas falls because the work done comes from its internal energy.","The temperature remains constant because no heat enters or leaves the gas.","The temperature rises because the gas does positive work.","The internal energy remains constant because $\\Delta Q = 0$."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In an adiabatic process $\\Delta Q = 0$, so $\\Delta W = -\\Delta U$.\nIn an expansion the gas does positive work, so $\\Delta U$ is negative.\nFor an ideal gas, U depends only on T, so the temperature falls.\nOption (2) is wrong because constant temperature describes an isothermal process, not $\\Delta Q = 0$. Option (4) is wrong because $\\Delta Q = 0$ does not make $\\Delta U = 0$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-062","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":62,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Raising the temperature of 2 mol of an ideal gas by a certain amount $\\Delta T$ requires 1000 J of heat at constant pressure. The same temperature rise requires only 600 J at constant volume. What is the molar specific heat of the gas at constant volume? (Take $R = 8.3$ J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$.)","image":"","options":["12.45 J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$","20.75 J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$","8.3 J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$","16.6 J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $Q_p = \\mu C_p\\Delta T = 1000$ J and $Q_v = \\mu C_v\\Delta T = 600$ J, with $\\mu = 2$.\nSubtracting: $Q_p - Q_v = \\mu(C_p - C_v)\\Delta T = \\mu R\\Delta T = 400$ J.\nSo $\\Delta T = \\frac{400}{2 \\times 8.3} \\approx 24.1$ K.\nThen $C_v = \\frac{Q_v}{\\mu\\Delta T} = \\frac{600 \\times 8.3}{400}$.\nSo $C_v = 12.45$ J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$ (that is, $1.5R$). The value 20.75 is $C_p$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-063","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":63,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"An ideal gas is enclosed in a rigid, sealed steel container. It is heated slowly using a burner. Which of the following correctly describes this process?","image":"","options":["Work done by the gas is zero, and all the heat supplied increases its internal energy","Work done by the gas equals the heat supplied, and its internal energy stays constant","The heat supplied is shared equally between work done and the increase in internal energy","The internal energy stays constant because the volume does not change"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A rigid container keeps the volume fixed, so the process is isochoric.\nWith $\\Delta V = 0$, the work done is $W = P\\Delta V = 0$.\nThe first law, $\\Delta Q = \\Delta U + \\Delta W$, then gives $\\Delta Q = \\Delta U$. All the heat raises the internal energy and the temperature.\nOption (2) describes an isothermal process, not an isochoric one. Option (4) wrongly links internal energy to volume; for an ideal gas, U depends on temperature, which rises here.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-064","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":64,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"A Carnot engine uses an ideal gas as the working substance and starts from state 1 at the source temperature $T_1$. Which sequence of steps correctly describes one complete Carnot cycle?","image":"","options":["Adiabatic expansion → isothermal expansion → adiabatic compression → isothermal compression","Isothermal expansion → isochoric cooling → isothermal compression → isochoric heating","Isothermal expansion → adiabatic expansion → isothermal compression → adiabatic compression","Isothermal expansion → adiabatic compression → isothermal compression → adiabatic expansion"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Step 1→2: isothermal expansion at $T_1$, during which heat $Q_1$ is absorbed.\nStep 2→3: adiabatic expansion, which cools the gas from $T_1$ to $T_2$.\nStep 3→4: isothermal compression at $T_2$, during which heat $Q_2$ is rejected.\nStep 4→1: adiabatic compression, which heats the gas from $T_2$ back to $T_1$.\nIsochoric steps would need a whole series of reservoirs, so they cannot appear in a two-reservoir reversible engine.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-065","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":65,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: When two gases are separated by an adiabatic wall and insulated from the surroundings, any possible pair of values $(P_A, V_A)$ is in equilibrium with any possible pair $(P_B, V_B)$.\nStatement II: If the adiabatic wall is replaced by a diathermic wall and both gases are in rigid containers of fixed volume, both the pressure and the volume of each gas must change to reach thermal equilibrium.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. An adiabatic wall allows no heat flow, so the states of A and B do not affect each other and any pairs of values can coexist.\nStatement II is incorrect. Both variables need not change; it depends on the constraints.\nWith fixed-volume containers, V cannot change, so only the pressures change while thermal equilibrium is reached.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-066","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":66,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"To prove Carnot's theorem, an irreversible engine I and a Carnot engine R work between the same source and sink. I runs as an engine: it takes heat $Q_1$ from the source and delivers work $W'$. R runs as a refrigerator: it returns the same $Q_1$ to the source and needs work $W$. Suppose $\\eta_I > \\eta_R$, so that $W' > W$. What is the net effect of one cycle of the combined system?","image":"","options":["Heat $W' - W$ flows from the sink to the source with no work, violating the Clausius statement","Heat $W' - W$ is taken from the sink and wholly converted into work, with no other change, violating the Kelvin–Planck statement","Work $W' - W$ is created from nothing, violating the first law of thermodynamics","Heat $W' - W$ is taken from the source and wholly converted into work, which is allowed because the source is hot"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The source gives $Q_1$ to I and receives $Q_1$ back from R, so it has no net change.\nThe sink receives $Q_1 - W'$ from I and gives up $Q_2 = Q_1 - W$ to R. Its net loss is $W' - W$.\nThe net work output is also $W' - W$, so energy is conserved and the first law holds.\nThe sole result is heat from one reservoir turned fully into work. This violates the Kelvin–Planck statement, so $\\eta_I > \\eta_R$ is impossible.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-067","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":67,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (process for an ideal gas) with List-II (feature of the process).\nList-I:\n(A) Isothermal\n(B) Isobaric\n(C) Isochoric\n(D) Adiabatic\nList-II:\n(i) No work is done by or on the gas\n(ii) No heat flows between the system and the surroundings\n(iii) $\\Delta U = 0$, so heat absorbed equals work done\n(iv) Work done $= P(V_2 - V_1)$","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Isothermal: T is constant. The internal energy of an ideal gas depends only on T, so $\\Delta U = 0$ and Q = W. This gives (iii).\n(B) Isobaric: P is constant, so the work done is $W = P(V_2 - V_1)$. This gives (iv).\n(C) Isochoric: V is constant, so no work is done. This gives (i).\n(D) Adiabatic: the system is insulated and Q = 0. This gives (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-068","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":68,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"A gas is taken from equilibrium state A to equilibrium state B adiabatically, and 50 J of work is done on the gas. The same gas is then taken from A to B along a different path, in which it absorbs 5.0 cal of heat. What is the net work done by the gas along the second path? (Take 1 cal = 4.2 J.)","image":"","options":["$-29$ J","29 J","71 J","$-71$ J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: on the adiabatic path, $\\Delta Q = 0$ and $\\Delta W = -50$ J. So $\\Delta U = \\Delta Q - \\Delta W = +50$ J.\nSince U is a state variable, $\\Delta U = 50$ J on the second path too.\nOn the second path, $\\Delta Q = 5.0 \\times 4.2 = 21$ J.\nFormula: $\\Delta W = \\Delta Q - \\Delta U = 21 - 50$.\nSo $\\Delta W = -29$ J, meaning 29 J of work is done on the gas.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-069","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":69,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Inside a sealed boiler, 3.0 g of water vaporises completely at a constant pressure of $2.0 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa. Under these conditions:\n- latent heat of vaporisation = 2200 J/g\n- volume of 1 g of liquid water = 1 cm$^3$\n- volume of 1 g of vapour = 900 cm$^3$\nWhat is the increase in internal energy of the water?","image":"","options":["6060.6 J","7139.4 J","6600 J","6330.3 J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\Delta Q = 3.0 \\times 2200 = 6600$ J.\nChange in volume: $\\Delta V = 3.0 \\times (900 - 1) = 2697$ cm$^3 = 2.697 \\times 10^{-3}$ m$^3$.\nWork done by the water: $W = P\\Delta V = 2.0 \\times 10^{5} \\times 2.697 \\times 10^{-3} = 539.4$ J.\nFirst law: $\\Delta U = \\Delta Q - W = 6600 - 539.4$.\nSo $\\Delta U = 6060.6$ J. Using atmospheric pressure ($10^5$ Pa) instead of the given pressure leads to 6330.3 J.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-070","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":70,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about reversible engines is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The efficiency of a Carnot engine is independent of the nature of the working substance","A process is reversible only if it is quasi-static and free of dissipative effects such as friction and viscosity","In a reversible engine working between only two reservoirs, isochoric steps could replace the adiabatic steps that change the temperature from $T_1$ to $T_2$","The relation $\\frac{Q_1}{Q_2} = \\frac{T_1}{T_2}$ for a Carnot cycle can define a universal thermodynamic temperature scale"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: this is part of Carnot's theorem.\n(2) Correct: reversibility needs quasi-static change and no dissipation.\n(3) Incorrect: a reversible isochoric change from $T_1$ to $T_2$ needs a series of reservoirs, so there is never a finite temperature difference. With only two reservoirs, the temperature must be changed adiabatically.\n(4) Correct: the relation is universal because it does not depend on the substance.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-071","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":71,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"A gas in a cylinder absorbs 500 J of heat from a hot body and does 200 J of work in pushing a piston outward. What is the change in its internal energy?","image":"","options":["300 J","700 J","$-300$ J","200 J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\Delta Q = +500$ J (heat absorbed), $\\Delta W = +200$ J (work done by the gas).\nFormula: $\\Delta U = \\Delta Q - \\Delta W$.\nSubstituting: $\\Delta U = 500 - 200 = 300$ J.\nSo the internal energy increases by 300 J. Adding the two values (700 J) would wrongly treat work done by the gas as energy given to it.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-072","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":72,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Three copper blocks of masses $M_1$, $M_2$ and $M_3$ kg respectively are brought into thermal contact till they reach equilibrium. Before contact, they were at $T_1$, $T_2$, $T_3$ ($T_1 > T_2 > T_3$). Assuming there is no heat loss to the surroundings, the equilibrium temperature $T$ is ($s$ is specific heat of copper)","image":"","options":["$T = \\frac{T_1 + T_2 + T_3}{3}$","$T = \\frac{M_1T_1 + M_2T_2 + M_3T_3}{M_1 + M_2 + M_3}$","$T = \\frac{M_1T_1 + M_2T_2 + M_3T_3}{3(M_1 + M_2 + M_3)}$","$T = \\frac{M_1T_1s + M_2T_2s + M_3T_3s}{M_1 + M_2 + M_3}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: copper blocks of masses $M_1, M_2, M_3$ at $T_1, T_2, T_3$, with no heat lost to the surroundings.\nPrinciple: heat lost by the hotter blocks equals heat gained by the cooler ones, so $\\sum M_i s(T_i - T) = 0$.\nSolving: $s(M_1T_1+M_2T_2+M_3T_3) = sT(M_1+M_2+M_3)$. The common $s$ cancels, giving $T = \\frac{M_1T_1+M_2T_2+M_3T_3}{M_1+M_2+M_3}$, a mass-weighted average.\nOption (1) ignores the masses. Option (4) is dimensionally wrong because $s$ does not cancel.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Physics Class 11, Ch 12 (MCQ Q12.6)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-073","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":73,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"A gas is taken from state A to state B along path 1. During this, it absorbs 500 J of heat and does 200 J of work on the surroundings. The gas is then returned from B to A along a different path 3, in which the surroundings do 150 J of work on the gas. How much heat does the gas release during path 3?","image":"","options":["450 J","150 J","300 J","650 J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: on path 1, $\\Delta Q = 500$ J and $\\Delta W = 200$ J (work by the gas). On path 3, $\\Delta W = -150$ J.\nFormula: $\\Delta U = \\Delta Q - \\Delta W$. U is a state variable, so $\\Delta U_{B\\to A} = -\\Delta U_{A\\to B}$.\nSubstituting: $\\Delta U_{A\\to B} = 500 - 200 = 300$ J, so $\\Delta U_{B\\to A} = -300$ J.\nThen $\\Delta Q = \\Delta U + \\Delta W = -300 + (-150) = -450$ J.\nSo the gas releases 450 J of heat.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-074","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":74,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When an ideal gas is compressed isothermally, it gives out heat to the surroundings.\nReason (R): In an isothermal compression of an ideal gas, $\\Delta U = 0$ and the work done by the gas is negative, so $Q = W$ is negative.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: the environment does work on the gas, but the temperature cannot rise, so energy leaves the gas as heat.\nR is true: U of an ideal gas depends only on T, so $\\Delta U = 0$. Also $W = \\mu RT\\ln\\frac{V_2}{V_1} < 0$ for $V_2 < V_1$.\nThe first law then gives $Q = W < 0$, which is exactly why heat is released. So R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-075","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":75,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"A Carnot engine uses 2 mol of an ideal gas between reservoirs at 600 K and 300 K. During the isothermal expansion the volume doubles. What is the net work done by the gas per cycle? (R = 8.31 J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$, ln 2 = 0.693)","image":"","options":["3456 J","6912 J","1728 J","4986 J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\mu$ = 2, $T_1$ = 600 K, $T_2$ = 300 K and $V_2/V_1$ = 2.\nThe two adiabatic steps give $V_3/V_4 = V_2/V_1$, and their work terms cancel.\nFormula: $W = \\mu R(T_1 - T_2)\\ln(V_2/V_1)$.\nSubstituting: W = 2 × 8.31 × 300 × 0.693.\nSo W ≈ 3456 J. Option (2) is $Q_1$, the heat absorbed; the efficiency here is 0.5, so W = $Q_1$/2.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-076","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":76,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"A fixed mass of an ideal gas is compressed quasi-statically and isothermally to half its original volume.\nStatement I: The pressure of the gas becomes twice its initial value.\nStatement II: During this compression, the gas releases heat to the surroundings.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. For an isothermal process, $PV$ = constant (Boyle's law). Halving V therefore doubles P.\nStatement II is correct. Since T is constant, $\\Delta U = 0$ for an ideal gas, so Q = W.\nIn a compression, W < 0 (work is done on the gas). Hence Q < 0, which means heat is released.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-077","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":77,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (process of an ideal gas) with List-II (characteristic).\nList-I:\n(A) Isothermal\n(B) Adiabatic\n(C) Isochoric\n(D) Cyclic\nList-II:\n(I) No heat exchange with the surroundings\n(II) No work done by or on the gas\n(III) $PV$ = constant\n(IV) Final state is the same as the initial state","image":"","options":["A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(A) Isothermal: T is fixed, so $PV$ = constant (Boyle's law). This gives A-III.\n(B) Adiabatic: the system is insulated, so Q = 0. This gives B-I.\n(C) Isochoric: V is constant, so W = 0. This gives C-II.\n(D) Cyclic: the system returns to its initial state. This gives D-IV.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-078","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":78,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, the internal energy $U$ of a gas enclosed in a box is best described as:","image":"","options":["The kinetic energy of the box moving as a whole plus the kinetic energy of its molecules","The sum of kinetic and potential energies of the molecules, measured in the frame in which the centre of mass of the gas is at rest","The total heat stored in the gas at a given temperature","Only the translational kinetic energy of the molecules, excluding rotational and vibrational motion"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Internal energy is the sum of the molecular kinetic and potential energies.\nIt is measured in the frame in which the centre of mass of the system is at rest. So it counts only the disordered motion of the molecules.\nThe kinetic energy of the box moving as a whole is excluded, so (1) fails.\nRotational and vibrational kinetic energies are included, so (4) fails.\n'Heat stored' is a meaningless idea, so (3) fails.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-079","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":79,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"A fixed mass of an ideal gas occupies 6.0 L at a pressure of 1.0 atm. It is compressed slowly to 2.0 L while its temperature is kept constant. What is the final pressure?","image":"","options":["3.0 atm","0.33 atm","2.0 atm","9.0 atm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $P_1 = 1.0$ atm, $V_1 = 6.0$ L, $V_2 = 2.0$ L, with T constant.\nFormula: for an isothermal process, $PV$ is constant (Boyle's law), so $P_1V_1 = P_2V_2$.\nSubstituting: $P_2 = \\frac{1.0 \\times 6.0}{2.0}$.\nSo $P_2$ = 3.0 atm. The pressure rises because the volume falls; 0.33 atm wrongly assumes P varies directly with V.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-080","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":80,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"3 g of water is boiled into steam at atmospheric pressure ($1.013 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa).\nLatent heat of vaporisation of water = 2256 J/g.\nIncrease in volume on vaporisation = 1670 cm$^3$ per gram.\nWhat is the increase in the internal energy of the water?","image":"","options":["About 6260 J","6768 J","About 508 J","About 7276 J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\Delta Q = 3\\times2256$ = 6768 J and $\\Delta V = 3\\times1670\\times10^{-6}$ m$^3$.\nFormula: $\\Delta U = \\Delta Q - P\\Delta V$.\nSubstituting: $P\\Delta V = 1.013\\times10^{5}\\times5.01\\times10^{-3}$ ≈ 508 J.\nSo $\\Delta U$ = 6768 − 508 ≈ 6260 J.\nMost of the heat goes into raising the internal energy, just as in the NCERT 1 g example.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-081","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":81,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"A gas starts at state D ($P = 5.0 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa, $V = 1.0$ L). It goes to state E ($P = 2.0 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa, $V = 5.0$ L) along a straight line on the P-V diagram. It is then compressed isobarically from E to F, back to a volume of 1.0 L. What is the total work done by the gas from D to E to F?","image":"","options":["600 J","1400 J","2200 J","−600 J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"D→E (linear): the work is the trapezium area under the line, $\\frac{1}{2}(5.0 + 2.0) \\times 10^5 \\times 4.0 \\times 10^{-3} = 1400$ J.\nE→F (isobaric compression): $W = P\\Delta V = 2.0 \\times 10^5 \\times (-4.0 \\times 10^{-3}) = -800$ J.\nTotal: $W = 1400 - 800$.\nSo W = 600 J. This equals the triangular area between the two paths. Adding magnitudes wrongly gives 2200 J.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-082","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":82,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"One mole of an ideal diatomic gas ($\\gamma = 1.4$) at 300 K is compressed adiabatically and quasi-statically to $\\frac{1}{32}$ of its initial volume. How much work is done on the gas? (Take $R = 8.3$ J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["18675 J","8630 J","11205 J","7470 J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\mu = 1$, $T_1 = 300$ K, $\\frac{V_1}{V_2} = 32$, $\\gamma = 1.4$.\nStep 1: $T_2 = T_1 (32)^{0.4} = 300 \\times 4 = 1200$ K.\nStep 2: Work done by the gas is $W = \\frac{\\mu R(T_1 - T_2)}{\\gamma - 1} = \\frac{8.3 \\times (-900)}{0.4} = -18675$ J.\nSo the work done on the gas is +18675 J.\nThe value 8630 J is the isothermal result $RT\\ln 32$, which does not apply here.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-083","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":83,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"While deriving $C_p - C_v = R$ for an ideal gas, the subscript in $\\left(\\frac{\\Delta U}{\\Delta T}\\right)_p$ is dropped and the term is replaced by $C_v$. Why is this step justified?","image":"","options":["Because the pressure of an ideal gas does not change when it is heated","Because the internal energy of an ideal gas depends only on its temperature","Because no work is done by an ideal gas when it is heated at constant pressure","Because $\\Delta Q$ is a state variable for an ideal gas"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"For an ideal gas, U is a function of temperature alone. It does not depend on P or V.\nSo for a given $\\Delta T$, $\\Delta U$ is the same whether the heating is at constant volume or at constant pressure. Hence $\\left(\\frac{\\Delta U}{\\Delta T}\\right)_p = \\left(\\frac{\\Delta U}{\\Delta T}\\right)_v = C_v$.\nOption (3) is wrong: at constant pressure the gas expands and does work $P\\Delta V$. That work is exactly the extra term R.\nOption (4) is wrong because heat is not a state variable.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-084","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":84,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"2 g of water is boiled at atmospheric pressure ($1.013 \\times 10^5$ Pa). The latent heat of vaporisation is 2256 J/g, and each gram expands from 1 $cm^3$ (liquid) to 1671 $cm^3$ (vapour). What is the increase in internal energy of the water?","image":"","options":["4174 J","4512 J","4850 J","4343 J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\Delta Q$ = 2 × 2256 = 4512 J and $\\Delta V$ = 2 × 1670 $cm^3$ = $3.34 \\times 10^{-3}$ $m^3$.\nFormula: $\\Delta U = \\Delta Q - P\\Delta V$.\nSubstituting: $P\\Delta V$ = $1.013\\times10^5 \\times 3.34\\times10^{-3}$ ≈ 338.3 J, so $\\Delta U$ = 4512 − 338.3.\nSo $\\Delta U$ ≈ 4174 J. Most of the heat goes into internal energy, only a small part into work.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-085","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":85,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When an ideal gas expands quasi-statically and isothermally, the heat absorbed by the gas equals the work done by it.\nReason (R): The internal energy of an ideal gas depends only on its temperature.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true. For an ideal gas, intermolecular forces are neglected, so U depends only on T.\nA is true. Since T is fixed, $\\Delta U = 0$, and the first law gives $\\Delta Q = \\Delta W$.\nR explains A directly: it is precisely because U depends only on T that $\\Delta U = 0$ in the isothermal process.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-086","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":86,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"A cylinder with insulating walls and an insulated piston contains a diatomic ideal gas ($\\gamma = 1.4$). The gas is compressed slowly to half its original volume. By what factor does its pressure increase?","image":"","options":["2.64","2.00","1.40","1.32"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: adiabatic process, $V_2 = \\frac{V_1}{2}$, $\\gamma = 1.4$.\nFormula: $P_1V_1^\\gamma = P_2V_2^\\gamma$, so $\\frac{P_2}{P_1} = \\left(\\frac{V_1}{V_2}\\right)^\\gamma$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{P_2}{P_1} = 2^{1.4}$.\nSo the pressure increases by a factor of about 2.64. The value 2 would be the isothermal (Boyle's law) result.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-087","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":87,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the heat capacity of a given sample of gas is correct?","image":"","options":["It depends on the process or conditions under which heat is supplied","It has a single fixed value that depends only on the mass of the gas","It is the same at constant pressure and at constant volume","It is independent of the amount of gas taken"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT stresses that heat capacity, in general, depends on the process the system goes through when heat is supplied.\nThis is why gases have two heat capacities, $C_p$ and $C_v$, and these are unequal.\nHeat capacity $S = \\frac{\\Delta Q}{\\Delta T}$ is proportional to the mass of the sample. Only the specific and molar values are independent of the amount.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-088","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":88,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"A gas-fired geyser heats water flowing at 4.0 litres per minute from 20 °C to 60 °C. The heat of combustion of the fuel is $5.0 \\times 10^{4}$ J/g. Assuming all the heat produced goes into the water, what is the rate of fuel consumption? (Specific heat of water = 4.2 J g$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$, density of water = 1 g cm$^{-3}$)","image":"","options":["3.4 g/min","8.4 g/min","13.4 g/min","22.3 g/min"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: water flow = 4.0 L/min = 4000 g/min, $\\Delta T$ = 40 K, s = 4.2 J g$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$.\nHeat needed per minute: $\\Delta Q = m s \\Delta T = 4000 \\times 4.2 \\times 40 = 6.72 \\times 10^{5}$ J/min.\nFuel rate = heat per minute ÷ heat of combustion.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{6.72 \\times 10^{5}}{5.0 \\times 10^{4}}$ = 13.44 g/min.\nSo the fuel is consumed at about 13.4 g/min.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-089","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":89,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is the Kelvin-Planck statement of the second law of thermodynamics?","image":"","options":["No process is possible whose sole result is the transfer of heat from a colder object to a hotter object.","Two systems in thermal equilibrium with a third system separately are in thermal equilibrium with each other.","No process is possible whose sole result is the absorption of heat from a reservoir and the complete conversion of this heat into work.","Heat supplied to a system equals the increase in its internal energy plus the work done by it."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Option (1) is the Clausius statement. It denies a perfect refrigerator.\nOption (2) is the zeroth law.\nOption (4) is the first law, $\\Delta Q = \\Delta U + \\Delta W$.\nOption (3) denies a perfect heat engine, which is the Kelvin-Planck statement.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-090","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":90,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"A gas-fired water heater warms water flowing at 4.0 litres per minute from 20 °C to 60 °C. Only 80% of the heat released by burning the fuel reaches the water. The heat of combustion of the fuel is $5.0 \\times 10^{4}$ J/g. Find the rate of fuel consumption. (Density of water = 1 kg/L, specific heat of water = 4186 J kg$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$.)","image":"","options":["16.7 g/min","13.4 g/min","20.9 g/min","8.4 g/min"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: 4.0 kg of water per minute, $\\Delta T$ = 40 K, efficiency 0.80, heat of combustion $5.0\\times10^4$ J/g.\nFormula: heat taken by water per minute = $m s \\Delta T$; heat released by fuel = $\\frac{m s \\Delta T}{0.8}$.\nSubstituting: $4.0 \\times 4186 \\times 40 = 669760$ J/min, and $\\frac{669760}{0.8} = 837200$ J/min.\nFuel rate = $\\frac{837200}{5.0\\times10^4}$ g/min.\nSo the fuel rate $\\approx$ 16.7 g/min. The value 13.4 g/min ignores the 80% efficiency.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-091","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":91,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When 1 g of water at 100 °C changes into steam at atmospheric pressure, most of the heat supplied goes into increasing the internal energy of the water.\nReason (R): The work done by the water in expanding against the atmosphere during vaporisation is only about 169 J, compared with a latent heat of 2256 J.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: $\\Delta U = 2256 - 169.2 = 2086.8$ J, which is about 92% of the heat supplied.\nR is true: $W = P(V_g - V_l) \\approx 1.013 \\times 10^{5} \\times 1671 \\times 10^{-6} \\approx 169$ J.\nR explains A: by the first law, $\\Delta U = \\Delta Q - P\\Delta V$. Since the work term is small, almost all the heat remains as internal energy.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-092","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":92,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, one calorie is the amount of heat required to raise the temperature of 1 g of water through which interval?","image":"","options":["From 0 °C to 1 °C","From 3.5 °C to 4.5 °C","From 14.5 °C to 15.5 °C","From 99 °C to 100 °C"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The specific heat of water varies slightly with temperature between 0 and 100 °C, as shown in Fig. 11.5.\nSo a precise definition of the calorie must name the exact 1 °C interval.\nNCERT fixes this interval as 14.5 °C to 15.5 °C.\nThe 3.5–4.5 °C option is a tempting wrong choice because water has its maximum density near 4 °C, but that fact is not used to define the calorie.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-093","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":93,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Using the sign convention $\\Delta Q = \\Delta U + \\Delta W$ (with $\\Delta Q$ = heat supplied to the system and $\\Delta W$ = work done by the system), which of the following statements is correct?","image":"","options":["A negative $\\Delta W$ means the system does work on the surroundings","If $\\Delta Q$ is negative and $\\Delta W = 0$, the internal energy of the system decreases","In an isothermal compression of an ideal gas, the gas absorbs heat","If a gas does positive work with no heat exchange, its internal energy increases"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: $\\Delta W < 0$ means work is done on the system.\n(2) Correct: with $\\Delta W = 0$, $\\Delta U = \\Delta Q < 0$, so U decreases.\n(3) Incorrect: in isothermal compression $\\Delta U = 0$ and $W < 0$, so $Q = W < 0$. Heat is released.\n(4) Incorrect: with $\\Delta Q = 0$, $\\Delta U = -\\Delta W < 0$, so U decreases.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-094","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":94,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Heat capacity depends on the process. Match the process undergone by one mole of an ideal gas (List-I) with its molar heat capacity for that process (List-II).\nList-I\n(P) Isochoric\n(Q) Isobaric\n(R) Isothermal\n(S) Adiabatic\nList-II\n(1) Zero\n(2) Infinite\n(3) $C_v$\n(4) $C_v + R$","image":"","options":["P-3, Q-4, R-2, S-1","P-3, Q-4, R-1, S-2","P-4, Q-3, R-2, S-1","P-1, Q-2, R-3, S-4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Molar heat capacity is $C = \\frac{\\Delta Q}{\\mu \\Delta T}$.\n(P) Isochoric: $\\Delta V = 0$, so $\\Delta Q = \\Delta U$ and C = $C_v$.\n(Q) Isobaric: part of the heat does work $P\\Delta V = R\\Delta T$ per mole, so C = $C_v + R = C_p$.\n(R) Isothermal: heat is absorbed but $\\Delta T = 0$, so C is infinite.\n(S) Adiabatic: $\\Delta Q = 0$ while T changes, so C = 0.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-095","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":95,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"700 J of heat is supplied at constant pressure to an ideal gas with $\\gamma = 1.4$. How much work is done by the gas in expanding?","image":"","options":["200 J","500 J","280 J","700 J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Q = 700 J at constant pressure, $\\gamma = 1.4$.\nFormula: $\\Delta U = \\mu C_v\\Delta T$ and $Q = \\mu C_p\\Delta T$, so $\\frac{\\Delta U}{Q} = \\frac{1}{\\gamma}$.\nSubstituting: $\\Delta U = \\frac{700}{1.4} = 500$ J.\nFrom the first law, $W = Q - \\Delta U = 700 - 500$.\nSo W = 200 J.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-096","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":96,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"An ideal gas undergoes a Carnot cycle 1→2 (isothermal at $T_1$), 2→3 (adiabatic), 3→4 (isothermal at $T_2$), 4→1 (adiabatic). How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The work done by the gas in step 2→3 equals the work done on the gas in step 4→1.\n(ii) $\\frac{V_3}{V_4} = \\frac{V_2}{V_1}$\n(iii) The net work per cycle is $\\mu R (T_1 - T_2) \\ln\\frac{V_2}{V_1}$.\n(iv) The internal energy of the gas does not change during step 1→2.\n(v) In step 4→1 the gas absorbs heat, which raises its temperature from $T_2$ to $T_1$.\n(vi) The efficiency would be higher if a monatomic gas were used instead of a diatomic gas.","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: both equal $\\frac{\\mu R(T_1 - T_2)}{\\gamma - 1}$.\n(ii) Correct: the two adiabatic relations $TV^{\\gamma-1}$ = constant give this ratio.\n(iii) Correct: $\\mu RT_1 \\ln\\frac{V_2}{V_1} - \\mu RT_2 \\ln\\frac{V_3}{V_4}$, combined with (ii).\n(iv) Correct: the step is isothermal, and U of an ideal gas depends only on T.\n(v) Incorrect: the step is adiabatic, so no heat flows. The work done on the gas raises T.\n(vi) Incorrect: Carnot efficiency does not depend on the working substance.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-097","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":97,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"A gas absorbs 100 cal of heat and does 120 J of work on its surroundings. What is the change in its internal energy? (Take 1 cal = 4.186 J)","image":"","options":["298.6 J","538.6 J","−20 J","418.6 J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\Delta Q$ = 100 cal = 418.6 J and $\\Delta W$ = 120 J (work done by the gas).\nFormula (first law): $\\Delta U = \\Delta Q - \\Delta W$.\nSubstituting: $\\Delta U$ = 418.6 − 120.\nSo $\\Delta U$ = 298.6 J, an increase.\nOption (3) comes from forgetting to convert calories into joules.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-098","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":98,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"An ideal gas in a perfectly insulated cylinder pushes a piston outward and does 150 J of work on the surroundings. What is the change in internal energy of the gas?","image":"","options":["$-150$ J","$+150$ J","0 J","$+300$ J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: insulated cylinder, so the process is adiabatic and $\\Delta Q = 0$; work done by the gas $\\Delta W = 150$ J.\nFormula: $\\Delta Q = \\Delta U + \\Delta W$.\nSubstituting: $0 = \\Delta U + 150$.\nSo $\\Delta U = -150$ J. The gas does the work out of its own internal energy.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-099","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":99,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The Zeroth Law was formulated by R.H. Fowler in 1931, after the first and second laws had already been stated and numbered.\n(ii) If two gases A and B are separated by an adiabatic wall (and insulated from the rest of the surroundings), any pair of values $(P_A, V_A)$ can be in equilibrium with any pair $(P_B, V_B)$.\n(iii) A diathermic wall is an insulating wall that does not allow heat to flow between two systems.\n(iv) When a system is in thermodynamic equilibrium, its molecules are also in mechanical equilibrium, with zero net force on each molecule.\n(v) If systems A and B are each separately in thermal equilibrium with system C, then $T_A = T_B$.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: Fowler stated the Zeroth Law in 1931, long after the first and second laws were numbered.\n(ii) Correct: an adiabatic wall allows no heat flow, so the states of A and B do not constrain each other.\n(iii) Incorrect: a diathermic wall is a conducting wall that allows heat flow. The insulating wall is the adiabatic wall.\n(iv) Incorrect: in thermodynamic equilibrium only the macroscopic variables are constant. The molecules keep moving randomly and are not in mechanical equilibrium.\n(v) Correct: $T_A = T_C$ and $T_B = T_C$, so $T_A = T_B$ (Zeroth Law).\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-100","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":100,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Two identical samples of an ideal gas start from the same state (P, V, T). One is compressed isothermally to volume $\\frac{V}{2}$; the other is compressed adiabatically to volume $\\frac{V}{2}$.\nStatement I: More work must be done on the gas in the adiabatic compression than in the isothermal compression.\nStatement II: In the adiabatic compression, the final temperature of the gas is lower than its initial temperature.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect.","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect.","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Both curves start at the same point. On compression, the adiabatic curve rises more steeply, so its pressure is higher at every intermediate volume.\nThe area under the curve, $\\int P\\,dV$, is therefore larger for the adiabatic path. So Statement I is correct.\nIn the adiabatic case, $\\Delta Q = 0$, so the work done on the gas raises U and T. So Statement II is incorrect.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-101","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":101,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A mixture of petrol vapour and air, at the instant it undergoes explosive combustion after being ignited by a spark, is in an equilibrium state with well-defined pressure and temperature.\nReason (R): During such an explosive reaction, the temperature and pressure of the mixture are not uniform throughout.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A is false. NCERT gives the explosive reaction as an example of a non-equilibrium state, which cannot be described by state variables.\nR is true. During the reaction, temperature and pressure differ from place to place. This is exactly why there is no single well-defined P or T.\nOnly later does the gas attain uniform T and P.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-102","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":102,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): At any point on the P-V diagram of an ideal gas, the adiabatic curve through that point is steeper than the isotherm through it.\nReason (R): The slope of an adiabatic curve, $\\frac{dP}{dV} = -\\gamma\\frac{P}{V}$, is $\\gamma$ times the slope of the isotherm, and $\\gamma > 1$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true but R is false.","A is false but R is true."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Isotherm: $PV$ = constant, which gives $\\frac{dP}{dV} = -\\frac{P}{V}$.\nAdiabatic: $PV^\\gamma$ = constant, which gives $\\frac{dP}{dV} = -\\gamma\\frac{P}{V}$.\nSince $\\gamma = \\frac{C_p}{C_v} > 1$, the adiabatic slope has the larger magnitude. So A is true.\nR is true and directly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-103","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":103,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Match each process in List-I with its correct feature in List-II (ideal gas).\nList-I:\n(A) Isothermal\n(B) Isobaric\n(C) Isochoric\n(D) Cyclic\nList-II:\n(i) $W = 0$ and $Q = \\Delta U$\n(ii) The system returns to its initial state; net Q equals the area enclosed on the P-V diagram\n(iii) Temperature is constant and $PV$ is constant\n(iv) $W = \\mu R(T_2 - T_1)$","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Isothermal: T is fixed, so $PV$ = constant (Boyle's law). This matches (iii).\n(B) Isobaric: $W = P(V_2 - V_1) = \\mu R(T_2 - T_1)$. This matches (iv).\n(C) Isochoric: there is no volume change, so $W = 0$ and $Q = \\Delta U$. This matches (i).\n(D) Cyclic: $\\Delta U = 0$, so net Q = net W = enclosed area. This matches (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-104","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":104,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"An ideal gas undergoes a Carnot cycle 1→2→3→4→1 between a source at $T_1$ and a sink at $T_2$. Here 1→2 is isothermal expansion, 2→3 adiabatic expansion, 3→4 isothermal compression and 4→1 adiabatic compression. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Heat is absorbed from the source only during step 1→2.\n(ii) The work done by the gas in 2→3 equals, in magnitude, the work done on the gas in 4→1.\n(iii) $\\frac{V_3}{V_4} = \\frac{V_2}{V_1}$\n(iv) The efficiency depends on the value of $\\gamma$ of the working gas.\n(v) $\\frac{Q_1}{Q_2} = \\frac{T_1}{T_2}$","image":"","options":["5","4","3","2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the adiabatic steps involve no heat, and 3→4 releases heat to the sink.\n(ii) Correct: both magnitudes equal $\\frac{\\mu R(T_1-T_2)}{\\gamma-1}$.\n(iii) Correct: this follows from $TV^{\\gamma-1}$ = constant applied to both adiabats.\n(iv) Incorrect: $\\eta = 1-\\frac{T_2}{T_1}$, which is independent of the working substance.\n(v) Correct: this is a universal relation for a Carnot cycle.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-105","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":105,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following quantities for a given mass of gas:\npressure, volume, temperature, internal energy, heat, work, mass\nHow many of these are thermodynamic state variables?","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A state variable depends only on the state of the system, not on the path taken to reach it.\nPressure, volume, temperature, internal energy and mass are all state variables. That makes 5.\nHeat and work are modes of energy transfer. Their values depend on the path, so they are not state variables.\nThe tempting choice 6 wrongly counts heat as something a system 'contains'.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-106","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":106,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Consider two containers A and B containing identical gases at the same pressure, volume and temperature. The gas in container A is compressed to half of its original volume isothermally while the gas in container B is compressed to half of its original value adiabatically. The ratio of final pressure of gas in B to that of gas in A is","image":"","options":["$2^{\\gamma-1}$","$\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{\\gamma-1}$","$\\left(\\frac{1}{1-\\gamma}\\right)^{2}$","$\\left(\\frac{1}{\\gamma-1}\\right)^{2}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: both gases start at $P$, $V$, $T$, and each is compressed to $V/2$.\nIsothermal (A): $PV$ = constant, so $P_A = P\\cdot\\frac{V}{V/2} = 2P$.\nAdiabatic (B): $PV^{\\gamma}$ = constant, so $P_B = P\\left(\\frac{V}{V/2}\\right)^{\\gamma} = 2^{\\gamma}P$.\nRatio: $\\frac{P_B}{P_A} = \\frac{2^{\\gamma}P}{2P} = 2^{\\gamma-1}$. Since $\\gamma>1$, this ratio is greater than 1, as expected: adiabatic compression also heats the gas.\nOption (2), $(1/2)^{\\gamma-1}$, is the inverse ratio $P_A/P_B$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Physics Class 11, Ch 12 (MCQ Q12.5)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-107","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":107,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Every quasi-static process is necessarily reversible.\nStatement II: The Kelvin-Planck and Clausius statements of the second law are completely equivalent.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. A reversible process must be quasi-static and also free of dissipative effects such as friction and viscosity. A quasi-static process with a rubbing piston is still irreversible.\nStatement II is correct. NCERT notes that the two statements can be proved completely equivalent: violating one would allow violating the other.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-108","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":108,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"An ideal diatomic gas ($\\gamma = 1.4$) is compressed quasi-statically and adiabatically to one-third of its initial volume. By what factor does its pressure increase?","image":"","options":["4.66","3.00","1.55","2.16"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $V_2 = \\frac{V_1}{3}$, $\\gamma = 1.4$.\nFormula: $P_1V_1^\\gamma = P_2V_2^\\gamma$, so $\\frac{P_2}{P_1} = \\left(\\frac{V_1}{V_2}\\right)^\\gamma$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{P_2}{P_1} = 3^{1.4} \\approx 4.66$.\nThe value 3 is the isothermal (Boyle's law) result. It is smaller because during adiabatic compression the temperature also rises.\nSo the pressure increases by a factor of about 4.66.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-109","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":109,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"A Carnot cycle is run in reverse as a refrigerator. Its inside is kept at −3 °C and the room is at 27 °C. What is its coefficient of performance, defined as heat extracted from the cold reservoir per unit work done on it?","image":"","options":["9","10","0.1","0.9"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $T_2 = 270$ K (cold) and $T_1 = 300$ K (hot).\nFormula: $\\alpha = \\frac{Q_2}{W} = \\frac{Q_2}{Q_1 - Q_2}$. With $\\frac{Q_1}{Q_2} = \\frac{T_1}{T_2}$, this becomes $\\alpha = \\frac{T_2}{T_1 - T_2}$.\nSubstituting: $\\alpha = \\frac{270}{300 - 270} = 9$.\nThe value 10 is $\\frac{T_1}{T_1 - T_2}$, which uses heat delivered to the room instead of heat extracted.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-110","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":110,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"2 mol of an ideal gas with $C_v = 20.8$ J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$ is heated at constant pressure so that its temperature rises by 10 K. How much heat is supplied? (Take $R = 8.3$ J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["416 J","582 J","166 J","748 J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $\\mu = 2$ mol, $C_v = 20.8$ J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$, $\\Delta T = 10$ K, $R = 8.3$ J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$.\nFormula: $C_p = C_v + R$ and $Q = \\mu C_p \\Delta T$.\nSubstituting: $C_p = 20.8 + 8.3 = 29.1$ J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$, so $Q = 2 \\times 29.1 \\times 10$.\nSo Q = 582 J. Using $C_v$ alone would give 416 J, which is the heat needed at constant volume.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-111","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":111,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"2500 J of heat is supplied to 2 moles of an ideal gas at constant pressure. Its temperature rises by 40 K. What is the increase in the internal energy of the gas? (Take $R = 8.3$ J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$.)","image":"","options":["1836 J","2500 J","664 J","3164 J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Q = 2500 J, $\\mu = 2$, $\\Delta T = 40$ K, pressure constant.\nFor an isobaric process, the work done by the gas is $W = P\\Delta V = \\mu R\\Delta T = 2 \\times 8.3 \\times 40 = 664$ J.\nFirst law: $\\Delta U = Q - W = 2500 - 664$.\nSo $\\Delta U$ = 1836 J.\nOption (4) wrongly adds the work instead of subtracting it.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-112","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":112,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A quasi-static process is, in principle, infinitely slow, so the system remains in thermal and mechanical equilibrium with its surroundings throughout.\nReason (R): In a quasi-static process, the pressure and temperature of the system are exactly equal to those of the surroundings at every stage, with no difference at all.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT describes a quasi-static process as infinitely slow, with the system in equilibrium with its surroundings at every stage.\nR is false. The pressure and temperature differences are infinitesimally small, not exactly zero.\nIf there were no difference at all, there would be no driving cause and no change would occur.\nFor example, heat is exchanged in a quasi-static isothermal process only because of an infinitesimal temperature difference between the system and the reservoir.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-113","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":113,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A bullet moving at very high speed after being fired is not at a higher temperature merely because of its high speed.\nReason (R): Temperature is related to the energy of the internal, disordered motion of molecules, not to the kinetic energy of the body's centre of mass.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Firing changes the mechanical state (kinetic energy) of the bullet, not its temperature.\nR is true. Internal energy, and hence temperature, involves only the random molecular motion measured in the centre-of-mass frame.\nThe kinetic energy of the bullet as a whole is excluded from internal energy, so R correctly explains A.\nThe bullet heats up only when it stops in wood and its kinetic energy turns into internal energy.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-114","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":114,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"A system goes from state A to state B along path 1, absorbing 80 J of heat and doing 30 J of work. It is then returned from B to A along a different path, during which the surroundings do 20 J of work on the system. How much heat is released by the system on the return path?","image":"","options":["30 J","50 J","70 J","90 J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Path 1: $\\Delta U_{AB} = \\Delta Q - \\Delta W$ = 80 − 30 = 50 J.\nReturn path: $\\Delta U_{BA}$ = −50 J, since $U$ is a state variable.\nWork done by the system on the return path: $\\Delta W$ = −20 J.\n$\\Delta Q = \\Delta U + \\Delta W$ = −50 − 20 = −70 J, so 70 J of heat is released.\nCheck: net heat over the cycle = 80 − 70 = 10 J, which equals net work = 30 − 20 = 10 J, as required for a cyclic process.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-115","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":115,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about the Carnot engine and Carnot's theorem.\n(i) The efficiency of a Carnot engine working between $T_1$ and $T_2$ is higher if the working substance is an ideal gas than if it is a real substance.\n(ii) In a Carnot cycle, $\\frac{Q_1}{Q_2} = \\frac{T_1}{T_2}$, a relation that can be used to define a universal temperature scale.\n(iii) The engine uses adiabatic steps to change temperature. An isochoric step would need a series of reservoirs between $T_2$ and $T_1$ to stay quasi-static.\n(iv) Suppose an irreversible engine were more efficient than a Carnot engine and were coupled to it run as a refrigerator. The combination would extract heat from the sink and convert it completely into work.\nWhich statements are correct?","image":"","options":["(ii), (iii) and (iv) only","(i), (ii) and (iii) only","(i) and (iv) only","(ii) and (iii) only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(i) Incorrect: Carnot efficiency $1-\\frac{T_2}{T_1}$ does not depend on the working substance. An ideal gas is used only because it makes the calculation easy.\n(ii) Correct: this is a universal relation, independent of the system, so it defines a thermodynamic temperature scale.\n(iii) Correct: with only two reservoirs, any finite temperature difference makes a step non-quasi-static. Only reversible adiabatic steps can change the temperature.\n(iv) Correct: the coupled system extracts heat W′ − W from the sink and converts it fully into work. This violates the Kelvin-Planck statement.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-116","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":116,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Two vessels of volumes $V$ and $2V$ contain the same ideal gas at the same temperature. Their pressures are $3.0\\times10^5$ Pa and $1.5\\times10^5$ Pa respectively. They are connected by a thin tube of negligible volume, and the temperature is kept unchanged. What is the final common pressure?","image":"","options":["$2.0\\times10^5$ Pa","$2.25\\times10^5$ Pa","$1.5\\times10^5$ Pa","$4.5\\times10^5$ Pa"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $P_1 = 3.0\\times10^5$ Pa in V, $P_2 = 1.5\\times10^5$ Pa in 2V, same T.\nThe number of moles is extensive, so the moles simply add: $\\mu_1 + \\mu_2 = \\frac{P_1V + P_2(2V)}{RT}$.\nFormula: final $P = \\frac{(\\mu_1+\\mu_2)RT}{3V} = \\frac{P_1V + 2P_2V}{3V}$.\nSubstituting: $P = \\frac{(3.0 + 3.0)\\times10^5}{3} = 2.0\\times10^5$ Pa.\nThe tempting value $2.25\\times10^5$ Pa is just the average of the two pressures. Pressure is intensive and does not add or average this way.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-117","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":117,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For a solid, the molar specific heat measured at constant pressure is very nearly equal to the change in its internal energy per mole per kelvin.\nReason (R): When a solid is heated, its change in volume is negligible, so the work term $P\\Delta V$ in $\\Delta Q = \\Delta U + P\\Delta V$ is very small.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT writes $\\Delta Q \\approx \\Delta U$ for a solid at constant pressure. This gives $C = \\frac{\\Delta U}{\\Delta T}$, which equals 3R from equipartition.\nR is true. The volume change of a solid on heating is negligible, so $P\\Delta V \\approx 0$.\nR is exactly the step used to drop the work term. So R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-118","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":118,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: For most solids at ordinary room temperature, the measured molar specific heat is close to 3R, as predicted by the equipartition of energy.\nStatement II: This agreement with 3R continues to hold at low temperatures, and carbon is a typical example of a solid that obeys it.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is true. Each atom behaves as a 3-D oscillator with average energy $3k_BT$, so U = 3RT per mole. Since $\\Delta V$ ≈ 0 for a solid, C = 3R, and this matches experiment at room temperature.\nStatement II is false. The agreement breaks down at low temperatures, and carbon is an exception, not a typical example.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-119","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":119,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"2 mol of an ideal gas is the working substance of a Carnot engine with $T_1 = 500$ K and $T_2 = 300$ K. During the isothermal expansion the volume doubles ($V_2 = 2V_1$). Find the net work done by the gas per cycle. (Take $R = 8.3$ J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$ and $\\ln 2 = 0.693$.)","image":"","options":["2301 J","5752 J","3451 J","1150 J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\mu = 2$, $T_1 = 500$ K, $T_2 = 300$ K, $\\frac{V_2}{V_1} = 2$.\nThe adiabatic relations give $\\frac{V_3}{V_4} = \\frac{V_2}{V_1} = 2$.\nFormula: $W = \\mu R T_1 \\ln\\frac{V_2}{V_1} - \\mu R T_2 \\ln\\frac{V_3}{V_4} = \\mu R(T_1 - T_2)\\ln 2$.\nSubstituting: $W = 2 \\times 8.3 \\times 200 \\times 0.693 \\approx 2301$ J.\nThe value 5752 J is $Q_1$ and 3451 J is $Q_2$; neither is the net work.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-120","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":120,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are extensive quantities?\nInternal energy $U$, volume $V$, pressure $P$, density $\\rho$, total mass $M$, temperature $T$, heat supplied $\\Delta Q$","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Test: imagine the system split into two equal halves. Quantities that get halved are extensive.\n$U$, $V$ and $M$ get halved, so they are extensive.\n$\\Delta Q$ is proportional to the mass of the system, so it is also extensive, even though Q is not a state variable.\n$P$, $\\rho$ and $T$ stay the same in each half, so they are intensive.\nThe count is 4.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-121","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":121,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"According to the NCERT distinction between heat and internal energy, which of the following statements is physically meaningful?","image":"","options":["A gas in a given state has a certain amount of internal energy.","A gas in a given state has a certain amount of heat.","A gas in a given state has a certain amount of work.","A gas in a given state has a certain amount of heat and work stored in it."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Internal energy is a state variable, so a definite value of it belongs to each state.\nHeat is energy in transit. Saying a gas 'contains' heat is as meaningless as saying it 'contains' work.\nWe can only say that heat is supplied to a system or that work is done by it.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-122","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":122,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The Zeroth Law of Thermodynamics was formulated by R.H. Fowler in 1931, after the first and second laws had already been stated and numbered.\nStatement II: The Zeroth Law leads directly to the concept of internal energy of a system.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I: Correct. Fowler stated the Zeroth Law in 1931, which is why it is numbered 'zero'.\nStatement II: Incorrect. The Zeroth Law implies that systems in thermal equilibrium share a common physical quantity. That quantity is temperature, not internal energy.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-123","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":123,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Match each process of an ideal gas in List-I with the relation it satisfies in List-II.\nList-I:\n(A) Isothermal expansion\n(B) Adiabatic expansion\n(C) Isochoric heating\n(D) Free expansion into vacuum in an insulated container\nList-II:\n(I) $\\Delta Q = \\Delta U$\n(II) $\\Delta Q = \\Delta W$, with $\\Delta U = 0$ and $\\Delta Q \\neq 0$\n(III) $\\Delta Q = \\Delta U = \\Delta W = 0$\n(IV) $\\Delta W = -\\Delta U$","image":"","options":["A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Isothermal: T is fixed, so $\\Delta U = 0$ and the heat absorbed equals the work done. This is II.\n(B) Adiabatic: $\\Delta Q = 0$, so $\\Delta W = -\\Delta U$. This is IV.\n(C) Isochoric: $\\Delta V = 0$, so $\\Delta W = 0$ and $\\Delta Q = \\Delta U$. This is I.\n(D) Free expansion: the gas is insulated ($\\Delta Q = 0$) and pushes against nothing ($\\Delta W = 0$), so $\\Delta U = 0$. This is III.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-124","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":124,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Imagine a system in equilibrium divided into two equal halves. Consider the following quantities:\npressure, temperature, density, internal energy, volume, total mass, heat $\\Delta Q$ needed for a given temperature rise\nHow many of these are intensive variables?","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A quantity is intensive if it stays the same in each half. It is extensive if it gets halved.\nPressure, temperature and density stay the same in each half. These are the 3 intensive quantities.\nInternal energy, volume and total mass are halved, so they are extensive.\n$\\Delta Q$ for a given temperature rise is proportional to mass, so it is also extensive, even though heat is not a state variable.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-125","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":125,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"The P-V diagram of a Carnot cycle using an ideal gas has four steps: 1→2 (isothermal at $T_1$), 2→3 (adiabatic), 3→4 (isothermal at $T_2$) and 4→1 (adiabatic), with $T_1 > T_2$. In which step is work done ON the gas while heat is RELEASED by the gas?","image":"","options":["1 → 2","2 → 3","3 → 4","4 → 1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"1→2 is isothermal expansion: the gas absorbs $Q_1$ and does work.\n2→3 is adiabatic expansion: Q = 0, and the gas does work.\n3→4 is isothermal compression at $T_2$: work is done on the gas and heat $Q_2$ is released to the sink.\n4→1 is adiabatic compression: work is done on the gas, but no heat is exchanged. This is the tempting wrong choice.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-126","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":126,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (process) with List-II (description).\nList-I:\n(A) Gas expanding into vacuum after a partition is suddenly removed\n(B) Gas in a metal cylinder kept in a large reservoir, expanding as the external pressure on a frictionless piston is lowered by tiny steps\n(C) Gas whose piston accelerates outward after the weight on it is suddenly lifted\n(D) Gas suddenly brought into contact with a reservoir much hotter than itself\nList-II:\n(I) Quasi-static; the system is in equilibrium at every stage\n(II) Non-equilibrium, caused by a finite pressure difference between gas and surroundings\n(III) Non-equilibrium, caused by a finite temperature difference with rapid heat exchange\n(IV) Non-equilibrium; there is no opposing external pressure and the gas pressure is non-uniform during expansion","image":"","options":["A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(A) Free expansion against vacuum has no opposing pressure, and the gas pressure is non-uniform during it, so A matches IV.\n(B) Infinitesimal pressure changes at constant reservoir temperature keep the gas in equilibrium at every stage, so B matches I (quasi-static).\n(C) Suddenly lifting the weight creates a finite pressure difference and the piston accelerates, so C matches II.\n(D) A finite temperature difference causes rapid heat exchange, so D matches III.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-127","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":127,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"According to the second law of thermodynamics, the coefficient of performance of a refrigerator:","image":"","options":["can never exceed 1","can never be infinite","must always equal the efficiency of a heat engine","must always be zero"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A refrigerator moves heat from a colder body to a hotter body using work done on it.\nAn infinite coefficient of performance would mean heat moves from cold to hot with zero work. That would be the sole result the Clausius statement forbids.\nSo the second law says the COP can never be infinite.\nNothing in the second law limits COP to 1. In practice, COP values greater than 1 are common.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-128","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":128,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"A Carnot engine absorbs 1000 J of heat in each cycle from a source at 500 K. It rejects heat to a sink at 300 K. How much heat does it reject to the sink per cycle?","image":"","options":["400 J","600 J","1000 J","1667 J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $Q_1 = 1000$ J, $T_1 = 500$ K, $T_2 = 300$ K.\nFormula: $\\frac{Q_1}{Q_2} = \\frac{T_1}{T_2}$ for a Carnot cycle.\nSubstituting: $Q_2 = 1000 \\times \\frac{300}{500} = 600$ J.\nSo heat rejected = 600 J. The value 400 J is the work output, not the heat rejected.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-129","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":129,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Two equal thermally insulated cylinders are connected by a stopcock. One contains an ideal gas and the other is evacuated. When the stopcock is suddenly opened, what happens to the temperature of the gas once equilibrium is reached?","image":"","options":["It remains unchanged.","It decreases, because the gas expands adiabatically.","It increases, because the gas rushes in rapidly.","It becomes half its initial value."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The system is insulated, so $\\Delta Q = 0$.\nThe gas expands against vacuum and pushes nothing, so $\\Delta W = 0$.\nBy the first law, $\\Delta U = 0$. For an ideal gas U depends only on T, so T is unchanged.\nOption (2) is tempting, but $PV^\\gamma$ = constant applies only to quasi-static adiabatic changes. Free expansion is not quasi-static.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-130","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":130,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Free expansion of a gas into vacuum is an irreversible process","A quasi-static isothermal expansion of an ideal gas in a cylinder with a frictionless piston is reversible","Cooking gas leaking from a cylinder and diffusing through a room is an irreversible process","Any quasi-static process is reversible, even if friction acts on the piston"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: free expansion takes the gas through non-equilibrium states, so it is irreversible.\n(2) Correct: NCERT cites this as an example of a reversible process.\n(3) Correct: diffusion does not reverse spontaneously.\n(4) Incorrect: reversibility needs the process to be quasi-static AND free of dissipative effects. Friction makes a process irreversible.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-131","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":131,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"A lead bullet moving at 300 m/s strikes a wooden block and comes to rest inside it. Assume 40% of the bullet's kinetic energy becomes internal energy of the bullet itself and the rest goes to the wood. The specific heat capacity of lead is 128 J kg$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$. What is the rise in temperature of the bullet? (Note: during flight its temperature was unaffected by its speed.)","image":"","options":["140.6 K","351.6 K","70.3 K","281.3 K"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: v = 300 m/s, fraction to bullet = 0.4, s = 128 J kg$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$.\nFormula: $0.4 \\times \\frac{1}{2} m v^2 = m s \\Delta T$, so $\\Delta T = \\frac{0.4 \\times v^2}{2s}$. The mass cancels.\nSubstituting: $\\Delta T = \\frac{0.4 \\times 90000}{2 \\times 128} = \\frac{18000}{128}$.\nSo the temperature rise is about 140.6 K. The ordered kinetic energy becomes disordered internal energy only when the bullet stops.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-132","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":132,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"2 mol of a monatomic ideal gas ($\\gamma = \\frac{5}{3}$) expands adiabatically, and its temperature falls from 400 K to 300 K. Find the work done by the gas. (Take R = 8.3 J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["1660 J","2490 J","4150 J","996 J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $\\mu = 2$, $T_1 = 400$ K, $T_2 = 300$ K, $\\gamma = \\frac{5}{3}$.\nFormula: $W = \\frac{\\mu R(T_1 - T_2)}{\\gamma - 1}$.\nSubstituting: $W = \\frac{2 \\times 8.3 \\times 100}{2/3} = 1660 \\times 1.5$.\nSo W = 2490 J. The value 1660 J comes from forgetting to divide by $\\gamma - 1$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-133","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":133,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"2 mol of a monatomic ideal gas ($C_v = \\frac{3}{2}R$) are heated at constant pressure. The temperature rises by 50 K. How much heat is supplied to the gas? (R = 8.3 J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["2075 J","1245 J","830 J","3320 J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\mu = 2$, $\\Delta T = 50$ K, $C_v = 1.5R$.\nWork by the gas: $W = \\mu R\\Delta T = 2 \\times 8.3 \\times 50 = 830$ J.\nChange in internal energy: $\\Delta U = \\mu C_v \\Delta T = 2 \\times 12.45 \\times 50 = 1245$ J.\nFirst law: $Q = \\Delta U + W = 1245 + 830$.\nSo Q = 2075 J. 1245 J is only $\\Delta U$, the answer if the process were isochoric.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-134","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":134,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"A gas is in an equilibrium state. Consider the following quantities that describe it:\npressure, internal energy, temperature, volume, density, total mass\nHow many of these are intensive variables?","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Test: imagine the system divided into two equal parts. A variable that stays the same in each part is intensive. A variable that is halved is extensive.\nPressure, temperature and density stay unchanged, so they are intensive.\nInternal energy, volume and total mass are halved, so they are extensive.\nThe intensive count is 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-135","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":135,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"An electric heater supplies heat to a gas at a steady rate of 250 W. The internal energy of the gas rises at a rate of 90 J/s, and the rest of the energy goes into work done by the gas. What total work does the gas do in 2 minutes?","image":"","options":["19.2 kJ","40.8 kJ","10.8 kJ","30.0 kJ"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\frac{dQ}{dt} = 250$ W, $\\frac{dU}{dt} = 90$ W, t = 120 s.\nFormula: $\\frac{dW}{dt} = \\frac{dQ}{dt} - \\frac{dU}{dt}$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{dW}{dt} = 250 - 90 = 160$ W, so $W = 160 \\times 120 = 19200$ J.\nSo W = 19.2 kJ.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-136","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":136,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"For a fixed amount of an ideal gas obeying $PV = \\mu RT$, how many of the variables P, V and T can be chosen independently?","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The equation of state $PV = \\mu RT$ connects P, V and T.\nFor a given $\\mu$, once any two of them are fixed (say P and V, or T and V), the third is determined by this equation.\nSo only two variables are independent.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-137","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":137,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"How much heat is needed to raise the temperature of 2.0 kg of water from 25 °C to 40 °C? (Specific heat capacity of water = 4186 J kg$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["62.8 kJ","125.6 kJ","251.2 kJ","334.9 kJ"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: m = 2.0 kg, s = 4186 J kg$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$, $\\Delta T$ = 15 K.\nFormula: $\\Delta Q = m s \\Delta T$.\nSubstituting: $\\Delta Q = 2.0 \\times 4186 \\times 15$ = 125580 J.\nSo $\\Delta Q \\approx$ 125.6 kJ. The value 62.8 kJ comes from forgetting the factor of mass.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-138","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":138,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (process) with List-II (defining condition).\nList-I:\n(A) Isothermal\n(B) Isobaric\n(C) Isochoric\n(D) Adiabatic\nList-II:\n(i) No heat flows between the system and the surroundings\n(ii) Volume remains constant\n(iii) Temperature remains constant\n(iv) Pressure remains constant","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(iii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Each process is defined by the quantity held fixed (Table 11.2):\nIsothermal: temperature is constant, so A-(iii).\nIsobaric: pressure is constant, so B-(iv).\nIsochoric: volume is constant, so C-(ii).\nAdiabatic: no heat flow, $\\Delta Q = 0$, so D-(i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-139","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":139,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"A gas is taken from state A to state B along several different paths.\nStatement I: The change in internal energy is the same for every path.\nStatement II: The heat absorbed by the gas is the same for every path.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. U is a state variable, so $\\Delta U$ depends only on the initial and final states.\nStatement II is incorrect. Heat is not a state variable, and $\\Delta Q$ generally depends on the path.\nThe same holds for work. Only the combination $\\Delta Q - \\Delta W$ is path independent.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-140","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":140,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"An ideal gas undergoes a Carnot cycle 1→2→3→4→1 between reservoirs at $T_1$ (hot) and $T_2$ (cold). Match each step in List-I with its feature in List-II.\nList-I:\n(a) 1→2\n(b) 2→3\n(c) 3→4\n(d) 4→1\nList-II:\n(P) Heat $Q_2$ is released to the cold reservoir\n(Q) Temperature falls from $T_1$ to $T_2$ with no heat exchange\n(R) Heat $Q_1$ is absorbed and all of it is converted to work by the gas\n(S) Work done on the gas raises its temperature from $T_2$ to $T_1$ with no heat exchange","image":"","options":["a-R, b-Q, c-P, d-S","a-R, b-S, c-P, d-Q","a-P, b-Q, c-R, d-S","a-Q, b-R, c-S, d-P"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) 1→2 is isothermal expansion at $T_1$. Since $\\Delta U = 0$, $Q_1 = W_{1\\to2}$, so it matches R.\n(b) 2→3 is adiabatic expansion, where temperature drops from $T_1$ to $T_2$. It matches Q.\n(c) 3→4 is isothermal compression at $T_2$, where heat $Q_2$ is released. It matches P.\n(d) 4→1 is adiabatic compression, where temperature rises back to $T_1$. It matches S.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-141","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":141,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A bullet moving at very high speed is not, because of that speed, at a higher temperature than an identical bullet at rest.\nReason (R): Temperature is related to the energy of the internal, disordered motion of the molecules and not to the motion of the body as a whole.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. The speed of the bullet changes its mechanical state (its kinetic energy), not its temperature.\nR is true. Internal energy and temperature come from random molecular motion measured in the centre-of-mass frame. The kinetic energy of the body as a whole is excluded.\nR directly explains why the bullet's speed does not raise its temperature.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-142","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":142,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"0.5 mol of an ideal gas with $\\gamma = \\frac{C_p}{C_v} = 1.4$ is heated at constant pressure. It absorbs 290.5 J of heat. What is the increase in its internal energy? (Take $R = 8.3$ J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["83.0 J","290.5 J","207.5 J","406.7 J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $\\mu = 0.5$, $\\gamma = 1.4$, $Q = 290.5$ J.\nFormula: from $C_p - C_v = R$ and $\\gamma = \\frac{C_p}{C_v}$, we get $C_v = \\frac{R}{\\gamma - 1} = 20.75$ and $C_p = 29.05$ J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$.\nSubstituting: $\\Delta T = \\frac{Q}{\\mu C_p} = \\frac{290.5}{0.5 \\times 29.05} = 20$ K.\nThen $\\Delta U = \\mu C_v \\Delta T = 0.5 \\times 20.75 \\times 20$.\nSo $\\Delta U$ = 207.5 J, which equals $\\frac{Q}{\\gamma}$. The value 83 J is the work done, $\\mu R \\Delta T$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-143","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":143,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Cylinder A contains an ideal gas at pressure $P_0$. It is connected through a closed stopcock to cylinder B, which is evacuated and has twice the volume of A. The whole system is thermally insulated. The stopcock is suddenly opened. After the gas settles to equilibrium, which statement is correct?","image":"","options":["The final pressure is $\\frac{P_0}{3}$ and the temperature of the gas is unchanged","The final pressure is $\\frac{P_0}{2}$ and the temperature of the gas falls","The gas does positive work $P_0 \\Delta V$, so its internal energy decreases","The final pressure is $\\frac{P_0}{3}$ and the temperature falls because the process is adiabatic"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The gas expands into vacuum, so there is nothing to push against and $\\Delta W = 0$.\nThe system is insulated, so $\\Delta Q = 0$.\nBy the first law, $\\Delta U = 0$. For an ideal gas U depends only on T, so T is unchanged.\nThe volume triples at the same temperature, so $P = \\frac{P_0}{3}$.\nFree expansion is not quasi-static, so $PV^\\gamma$ = constant does not apply and no cooling occurs.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-144","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":144,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"In 1798 Count Rumford studied the boring of a brass cannon. Which observation made the caloric (fluid) theory of heat untenable?","image":"","options":["The heat produced was enough to boil water","The heat produced depended on the work done by the horses turning the drill, not on the sharpness of the drill","A sharper drill produced much more heat than a blunt drill","The brass cannon lost mass as heat flowed out of it"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Caloric picture: a sharper drill should scoop more heat fluid out of the pores, so it should give more heat.\nObservation: the heat produced depended only on the work done by the horses, not on the drill's sharpness.\nThis shows that work is converted into heat, so heat is a form of energy.\nOption (1) is true, but boiling water alone does not decide between the two theories. Option (3) is the caloric prediction, which was not observed.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-145","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":145,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about the prediction C = 3R for solids:\n(i) It assumes that each atom behaves as an oscillator in three dimensions with average energy $k_BT$ per dimension.\n(ii) It uses the fact that $\\Delta V$ is negligible for a solid, so $\\Delta Q \\approx \\Delta U$ at constant pressure.\n(iii) Carbon is an exception to the prediction at ordinary temperatures.\n(iv) The agreement with experiment improves as the temperature is lowered.\nHow many of these statements are correct?","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: a 1-D oscillator has average energy $2 \\times \\frac{1}{2}k_BT = k_BT$, so 3-D gives $3k_BT$.\n(ii) Correct: for a solid $P\\Delta V$ is negligible, so $C = \\frac{\\Delta U}{\\Delta T} = 3R$.\n(iii) Correct: NCERT names carbon as the exception.\n(iv) Incorrect: the agreement breaks down at low temperatures; it does not improve.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-146","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":146,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): On a P-V diagram, the adiabatic curve passing through a given state of an ideal gas is steeper than the isotherm passing through the same state.\nReason (R): When an ideal gas is compressed adiabatically, its temperature rises, so for the same decrease in volume its pressure rises more than in an isothermal compression.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. For an isotherm, $PV$ = constant. For an adiabat, $PV^\\gamma$ = constant with $\\gamma > 1$, so P changes faster with V.\nR is true. In adiabatic compression $Q = 0$, so the work done on the gas increases U and therefore T.\nThe extra pressure comes both from the smaller volume and from the higher temperature. This is exactly why the adiabat is steeper, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-147","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":147,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"A gas in a cylinder with a frictionless piston expands slowly at a constant pressure of $2.0 \\times 10^5$ Pa. Its volume increases from 2.0 L to 5.0 L. What is the work done by the gas?","image":"","options":["600 J","1000 J","1400 J","$6.0 \\times 10^5$ J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $P = 2.0 \\times 10^5$ Pa, $V_1 = 2.0$ L, $V_2 = 5.0$ L.\nThe change in volume is $\\Delta V = 3.0$ L $= 3.0 \\times 10^{-3}$ m$^3$.\nFormula (isobaric process): $W = P(V_2 - V_1)$.\nSubstituting: $W = 2.0 \\times 10^5 \\times 3.0 \\times 10^{-3}$.\nSo W = 600 J. Option (4) comes from forgetting to convert litres to m$^3$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-148","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":148,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following thermodynamic variables is intensive?","image":"","options":["Internal energy $U$","Volume $V$","Density $\\rho$","Total mass $M$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Imagine dividing a system in equilibrium into two equal halves.\nVariables whose value is halved in each part are extensive. Internal energy, volume and total mass all get halved.\nDensity is mass divided by volume. Both are halved, so $\\rho$ stays the same in each half. Density is therefore intensive, like $P$ and $T$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-149","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":149,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"A gas expands slowly at a constant pressure of $2.0 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa. Its volume increases from 2.0 L to 5.0 L. How much work is done by the gas?","image":"","options":["600 J","0.6 J","6000 J","$1.0 \\times 10^{3}$ J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $P = 2.0 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa, $\\Delta V = 5.0 - 2.0 = 3.0$ L $= 3.0 \\times 10^{-3}$ m$^3$.\nFormula: $W = P\\Delta V$ (valid at constant pressure).\nSubstituting: $W = 2.0 \\times 10^{5} \\times 3.0 \\times 10^{-3}$.\nSo W = 600 J. Using the final volume instead of the change in volume gives the wrong value of 1000 J.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-150","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":150,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"For a Carnot engine with an ideal gas working between a hot reservoir at $T_1$ and a cold reservoir at $T_2$, match List-I (step) with List-II (what happens).\nList-I:\n(A) Step 1→2\n(B) Step 2→3\n(C) Step 3→4\n(D) Step 4→1\nList-II:\n(i) Heat $Q_2$ is released to the cold reservoir as the gas is compressed at constant temperature\n(ii) Gas expands with no heat exchange and its temperature falls from $T_1$ to $T_2$\n(iii) Work $\\frac{\\mu R(T_1 - T_2)}{\\gamma - 1}$ is done on the gas and its temperature rises to $T_1$\n(iv) Heat $Q_1 = \\mu R T_1 \\ln\\frac{V_2}{V_1}$ is absorbed from the hot reservoir","image":"","options":["A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(A) 1→2 is an isothermal expansion at $T_1$. The gas absorbs $Q_1 = \\mu R T_1 \\ln(V_2/V_1)$, so this is (iv).\n(B) 2→3 is an adiabatic expansion. The gas does work and cools from $T_1$ to $T_2$, so this is (ii).\n(C) 3→4 is an isothermal compression at $T_2$. Heat $Q_2$ is released to the sink, so this is (i).\n(D) 4→1 is an adiabatic compression. Work $\\mu R(T_1-T_2)/(\\gamma-1)$ is done on the gas, which heats to $T_1$, so this is (iii).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-151","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":151,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Stirring a liquid that is in thermal contact with a reservoir is an irreversible process.\nReason (R): Exactly reversing this process would amount to converting heat completely into work, with no other effect.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. The work done in stirring becomes internal energy of the reservoir.\nR is true. Reversing the process would require taking that heat back from the reservoir and turning it entirely into work, with nothing else changing.\nThat result is forbidden by the Kelvin-Planck statement. R therefore explains exactly why A holds.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-152","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":152,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are thermodynamic state variables?\nPressure, internal energy, heat supplied to the system, work done by the system, temperature, volume","image":"","options":["2","3","4","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A state variable depends only on the state of the system, not on how that state was reached.\nPressure, internal energy, temperature and volume are state variables.\nHeat and work are not state variables. They are modes of energy transfer, and their values depend on the path taken.\nCount = 4.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-153","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":153,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The heat capacity S of a body is independent of its mass.\n(ii) The SI unit of specific heat capacity is J kg$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$.\n(iii) Molar specific heat capacity C does not depend on the amount of substance taken.\n(iv) For gases, the specific heat capacity depends on the conditions (such as constant pressure or constant volume) under which heat is supplied.\n(v) The mechanical equivalent of heat is a fundamental physical constant, distinct from a mere unit conversion factor.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Incorrect: $S = \\frac{\\Delta Q}{\\Delta T}$ is proportional to the mass of the substance.\n(ii) Correct: $s = \\frac{1}{m}\\frac{\\Delta Q}{\\Delta T}$ has the unit J kg$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$.\n(iii) Correct: like s, C is a property of the substance and does not depend on its amount.\n(iv) Correct: gases have separate values $C_p$ and $C_v$.\n(v) Incorrect: NCERT calls it just a conversion factor between calorie and joule, and the term is superfluous in SI units.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-154","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":154,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"A fixed mass of gas is taken from state X to state Y along two different paths.\nStatement I: The change in internal energy of the gas is the same for both paths.\nStatement II: The heat absorbed by the gas is the same for both paths.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is true. U is a state variable, so $\\Delta U$ depends only on the initial and final states.\nStatement II is false. Heat and work are modes of energy transfer, not state variables, and in general depend on the path.\nOnly their difference $\\Delta Q - \\Delta W$ (which equals $\\Delta U$) is path-independent.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-155","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":155,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In a quasi-static process, the pressure of the system differs from the external pressure only by an infinitesimal amount at every stage.\nStatement II: A truly quasi-static process can be completed exactly in a finite, short time in a laboratory.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. In a quasi-static process, both the pressure difference and the temperature difference between system and surroundings are infinitesimal, so the system stays in equilibrium.\nStatement II is wrong. Such a process is, in principle, infinitely slow and is a hypothetical construct.\nReal slow processes, without accelerated piston motion or large temperature gradients, only approximate it.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-156","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":156,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): No engine working between two given reservoirs can have an efficiency greater than that of a Carnot engine working between the same reservoirs.\nReason (R): If an irreversible engine I were more efficient, coupling it to a Carnot engine run as a refrigerator would extract a net heat $W' - W$ from the sink and convert it completely into work, violating the Kelvin-Planck statement.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: this is part (a) of Carnot's theorem.\nR is true: suppose $\\eta_I > \\eta_R$. Let I drive R, which acts as a refrigerator returning the same $Q_1$ to the source.\nThe combined system then takes net heat $W' - W$ from the sink and turns all of it into work, with no other change.\nThis contradicts the Kelvin-Planck statement, so the supposition must be false. R is exactly the proof of A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-157","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":157,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"A gas kept at a constant pressure of $2.0 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa expands from 3.0 L to 5.0 L while absorbing 1000 J of heat. What is the change in its internal energy?","image":"","options":["1400 J","1000 J","400 J","600 J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: $P = 2.0\\times10^{5}$ Pa, $\\Delta V = 2.0\\times10^{-3}$ m$^3$, $\\Delta Q$ = 1000 J.\nFormula: $\\Delta W = P\\Delta V$ and $\\Delta U = \\Delta Q - \\Delta W$.\nSubstituting: $\\Delta W = 2.0\\times10^{5}\\times2.0\\times10^{-3}$ = 400 J.\nSo $\\Delta U$ = 1000 − 400 = 600 J.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-158","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":158,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In the isothermal expansion of an ideal gas, all the heat absorbed is converted into work, so this process violates the Kelvin-Planck statement of the second law.\nStatement II: The Kelvin-Planck statement and the Clausius statement of the second law are completely equivalent.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I: Incorrect. Kelvin-Planck forbids a process whose SOLE result is complete conversion of heat into work. In isothermal expansion, the gas's volume and pressure also change, so heat-to-work is not the only result and there is no violation.\nStatement II: Correct. NCERT states that the two forms can be proved equivalent.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-159","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":159,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"The Zeroth Law of Thermodynamics leads to the concept of which physical quantity, defined as the quantity that has the same value for two systems in thermal equilibrium?","image":"","options":["Internal energy","Heat","Pressure","Temperature"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The Zeroth Law says two systems that are each in thermal equilibrium with a third system are in thermal equilibrium with each other.\nThis implies there is a variable with the same value for systems in thermal equilibrium. That variable is called temperature T.\nPressure or internal energy need not be equal for two systems in thermal equilibrium.\nHeat is energy in transit, not a property of a state.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-160","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":160,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Using the law of equipartition of energy, what is the predicted molar specific heat capacity of a solid at ordinary temperatures? (Take R = 8.31 J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["8.3 J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$","12.5 J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$","16.6 J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$","24.9 J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Each atom vibrates in 3 dimensions, with average energy $k_BT$ per dimension, so its average energy is $3k_BT$.\nFor one mole: $U = 3k_BT \\times N_A = 3RT$.\nFor a solid $\\Delta V$ is negligible, so $C = \\frac{\\Delta U}{\\Delta T} = 3R$.\nSubstituting: $3 \\times 8.31$ = 24.93 J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$, i.e. about 24.9 J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-161","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":161,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following quantities are thermodynamic state variables?\nPressure, volume, temperature, internal energy, heat, work","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Pressure, volume and temperature depend only on the state of the system, so they are state variables.\nInternal energy also depends only on the state, not on the path, so it is a state variable.\nHeat and work are modes of energy transfer and depend on the path, so they are not state variables.\nThat gives 4 state variables.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-162","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":162,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When a gas is taken from state 1 to state 2, the heat absorbed by the gas is the same whichever path is followed.\nReason (R): Internal energy is a state variable, so $\\Delta U$ between two given states does not depend on the path.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true but R is false.","A is false but R is true."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"R is true: $U$ depends only on the state, so $\\Delta U$ is fixed for given end states.\nA is false: work done ($\\Delta W$) depends on the path. Since $\\Delta Q = \\Delta U + \\Delta W$, the heat absorbed also depends on the path.\nOnly the combination $\\Delta Q - \\Delta W$ is path independent.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-163","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":163,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Two identical gas samples X and Y start in the same equilibrium state and end in the same final equilibrium state at a higher temperature. X is in a rigid, thermally insulated vessel and is warmed by vigorously stirring it with a paddle wheel. Y is in a rigid conducting vessel and is warmed by placing it over a flame. Which statement is correct?","image":"","options":["X received heat, because its temperature rose","In the final state, Y contains more heat than X, because Y was heated by a flame","Both have the same change in internal energy; X gained energy as work and Y gained it as heat","X has a larger change in internal energy, because work transfers energy more efficiently than heat"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Both samples have the same initial and final states. Since U is a state variable, $\\Delta U$ is equal for both, so (4) fails.\nX is insulated, so no heat flows into it. The paddle does work on the gas, and a rise in temperature does not imply heat flow. So (1) fails.\nY gains energy by heat, driven by the temperature difference with the flame.\nSaying a state 'contains heat' is meaningless, because heat is energy in transit. So (2) fails.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-164","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":164,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"2 mol of an ideal gas expand isothermally and quasi-statically at 300 K, from 10 L to 20 L. What work is done by the gas? (Take R = 8.3 J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$, $\\ln 2 = 0.693$.)","image":"","options":["3.45 kJ","1.50 kJ","4.98 kJ","1.73 kJ"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\mu = 2$, T = 300 K, $\\frac{V_2}{V_1} = 2$.\nFormula: $W = \\mu RT \\ln\\frac{V_2}{V_1}$.\nSubstituting: $W = 2 \\times 8.3 \\times 300 \\times 0.693 \\approx 3452$ J.\nSo W ≈ 3.45 kJ. Using $\\log_{10}$ instead of ln gives 1.50 kJ, a common error.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-165","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":165,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (process for an ideal gas) with List-II (feature).\nList-I:\n(A) Isochoric\n(B) Adiabatic\n(C) Isothermal\n(D) Isobaric\nList-II:\n(i) Work done by gas $= \\mu R(T_2-T_1)$\n(ii) Heat absorbed goes entirely into changing internal energy\n(iii) No heat exchange; work done by the gas lowers its temperature\n(iv) Heat absorbed equals work done by the gas","image":"","options":["A-ii, B-iii, C-iv, D-i","A-iv, B-iii, C-ii, D-i","A-ii, B-i, C-iv, D-iii","A-i, B-iii, C-iv, D-ii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Isochoric: volume is fixed, so W = 0 and ΔQ = ΔU. This matches (ii).\n(B) Adiabatic: Q = 0, so work done by the gas comes from U and the temperature falls. This matches (iii).\n(C) Isothermal: for an ideal gas, U depends only on T, so ΔU = 0 and Q = W. This matches (iv).\n(D) Isobaric: $W = P(V_2-V_1) = \\mu R(T_2-T_1)$. This matches (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-166","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":166,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In SI units, the term 'mechanical equivalent of heat' is superfluous.\nReason (R): The mechanical equivalent of heat is merely a conversion factor between two units of energy, the calorie and the joule, and in SI the joule is used for heat as well as work.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that the term is superfluous and need not be used when heat is measured in joules.\nR is true. The mechanical equivalent of heat (about 4.186 J per cal) is only a factor for converting calories to joules.\nR explains A: once heat, work and all other forms of energy are expressed in joules, no separate conversion factor is needed.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-167","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":167,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Match each process of a gas in List-I with its first-law description in List-II.\nList-I:\n(A) Isochoric heating\n(B) Isothermal expansion of an ideal gas\n(C) Adiabatic compression\n(D) Free expansion of an ideal gas into vacuum in an insulated box\nList-II:\n(I) $\\Delta Q = 0$, $\\Delta W < 0$, $\\Delta U > 0$\n(II) $\\Delta W = 0$, $\\Delta U = \\Delta Q > 0$\n(III) $\\Delta U = 0$, $\\Delta Q = \\Delta W > 0$\n(IV) $\\Delta Q = 0$, $\\Delta W = 0$, $\\Delta U = 0$","image":"","options":["A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV","A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(A) Volume is constant, so no work is done; all the heat raises $U$. This is II.\n(B) $T$ is constant, so $\\Delta U = 0$ for an ideal gas; the heat absorbed equals the work done by the gas. This is III.\n(C) No heat flows and work is done on the gas, so $U$ rises. This is I.\n(D) The box is insulated and the gas pushes against nothing, so $Q = W = 0$ and hence $\\Delta U = 0$. This is IV.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-168","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":168,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A bullet fired from a gun is not at a higher temperature merely because it moves at high speed.\nReason (R): Temperature is related to the energy of the internal, disordered motion of the molecules, not to the motion of the body as a whole.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Firing the bullet changes its mechanical state (its kinetic energy), not its temperature.\nR is true. Temperature depends on the internal disordered energy of the molecules, not on the kinetic energy of the centre of mass.\nR explains A directly: the ordered motion of the whole bullet does not count towards its temperature.\nOnly when the bullet stops in wood is this kinetic energy converted into heat.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-169","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":169,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following correctly describes the formulation and naming of the Zeroth Law of Thermodynamics?","image":"","options":["It was formulated by Sadi Carnot in 1824, before the first and second laws","It was formulated by R.H. Fowler in 1931, long after the first and second laws had been stated and numbered","It was formulated by Count Rumford in 1798 as part of the cannon-boring experiment","It was formulated by Kelvin and Planck together with the second law"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that R.H. Fowler formulated this law in 1931.\nBy then, the first and second laws had already been stated and numbered.\nThe law is more basic than both because it underlies the idea of temperature, so it was called the 'zeroth' law.\nCarnot worked on heat engine efficiency, Rumford on heat as energy, and Kelvin–Planck on the second law.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-170","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":170,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"In a Rumford-type experiment, a drill driven at a steady 500 W bores into a 10 kg brass block for 5.0 minutes. Of the work done, 60% ends up as internal energy of the brass; the rest is lost to the surroundings. Taking the specific heat capacity of brass as 380 J kg$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$, what is the rise in temperature of the block?","image":"","options":["15.8 K","23.7 K","39.5 K","47.4 K"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: P = 500 W, t = 300 s, retained fraction = 0.60, m = 10 kg, s = 380 J kg$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$.\nWork done: W = Pt = 500 × 300 = $1.5 \\times 10^{5}$ J.\nEnergy retained by the brass: $\\Delta Q = 0.60 \\times 1.5 \\times 10^{5} = 9.0 \\times 10^{4}$ J.\nFormula: $\\Delta T = \\frac{\\Delta Q}{ms}$.\nSubstituting: $\\Delta T = \\frac{9.0 \\times 10^{4}}{10 \\times 380}$ ≈ 23.7 K. Ignoring the 60% factor would give 39.5 K.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-171","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":171,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"A Carnot cycle is run in reverse as a refrigerator. It removes heat from a freezer at $-13$ °C and rejects heat to a room at 27 °C. How much work must be done on it to remove 1300 J of heat from the freezer?","image":"","options":["173.3 J","200 J","1500 J","8450 J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $T_2$ = 260 K, $T_1$ = 300 K, $Q_2$ = 1300 J.\nFormula: for the reversed Carnot cycle, $\\frac{Q_1}{Q_2} = \\frac{T_1}{T_2}$, so the coefficient of performance is $\\alpha = \\frac{Q_2}{W} = \\frac{T_2}{T_1 - T_2}$.\nSubstituting: $\\alpha = \\frac{260}{40} = 6.5$, so $W = \\frac{1300}{6.5}$.\nSo W = 200 J. The heat rejected to the room is then $Q_1$ = 1500 J.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-172","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":172,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"A gas in a cylinder is slowly compressed by pushing the piston in. During the compression the gas also gives out heat to the surroundings. Using the NCERT sign convention in $\\Delta Q = \\Delta U + \\Delta W$, which is correct?","image":"","options":["$\\Delta Q > 0$ and $\\Delta W > 0$","$\\Delta Q < 0$ and $\\Delta W < 0$","$\\Delta Q < 0$ and $\\Delta W > 0$","$\\Delta Q > 0$ and $\\Delta W < 0$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Convention: $\\Delta Q$ is the heat supplied to the system, and $\\Delta W$ is the work done by the system.\nHeat leaves the gas, so $\\Delta Q < 0$.\nThe gas is compressed, so work is done on it, not by it. Hence $\\Delta W < 0$.\nOption (3) wrongly treats the work done on the gas as positive.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-173","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":173,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"A gas in equilibrium is described by the following quantities: pressure, temperature, density, internal energy, volume and total mass. How many of these are intensive variables?","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Test: divide the system into two equal halves. Variables that stay unchanged in each half are intensive.\nPressure, temperature and density are unchanged in each half, so they are intensive.\nInternal energy, volume and total mass are halved in each half, so they are extensive.\nCount of intensive variables = 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-174","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":174,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"On a P-V diagram (V on the horizontal axis), an ideal gas is taken around a rectangular cycle. The two isobaric sides are at $1.0 \\times 10^5$ Pa and $3.0 \\times 10^5$ Pa. The two isochoric sides are at 1.0 L and 4.0 L. The cycle is traversed anticlockwise. What is the net heat absorbed by the gas in one cycle?","image":"","options":["600 J","$-600$ J","1200 J","0 J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In a cyclic process, $\\Delta U = 0$, so the net heat absorbed equals the net work done by the gas.\nThe magnitude of the net work is the enclosed area: $(3.0 - 1.0) \\times 10^5 \\times (4.0 - 1.0) \\times 10^{-3} = 600$ J.\nAnticlockwise traversal means the compression occurs at the higher pressure. Net work is therefore done on the gas, so W = −600 J.\nSo Q = W = −600 J; the gas gives out 600 J of heat per cycle.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-175","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":175,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about heat capacity S and specific heat capacity s is correct?","image":"","options":["Specific heat capacity s of a substance is independent of its temperature","Heat capacity S is proportional to the mass of the substance, while s is independent of the amount of substance","The SI unit of specific heat capacity is J K$^{-1}$","Molar specific heat capacity C becomes larger when more moles of the substance are taken"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"$S = \\frac{\\Delta Q}{\\Delta T}$ is proportional to mass. Dividing by mass gives $s = \\frac{S}{m}$, which does not depend on the amount of substance. So option 2 is correct.\nOption 1 is wrong: s depends on the nature of the substance and also on its temperature.\nOption 3 is wrong: the unit of s is J kg$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$; J K$^{-1}$ is the unit of S.\nOption 4 is wrong: C is defined per mole, so it is independent of the amount.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-176","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":176,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Match the process for an ideal gas in List-I with its molar heat capacity in List-II.\nList-I:\n(a) Isochoric\n(b) Isobaric\n(c) Isothermal\n(d) Adiabatic\nList-II:\n(i) Zero\n(ii) Infinite\n(iii) $C_v$\n(iv) $C_p$","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Use $C = \\frac{1}{\\mu}\\frac{\\Delta Q}{\\Delta T}$ for each process.\n(a) Isochoric: by definition C = $C_v$.\n(b) Isobaric: by definition C = $C_p$.\n(c) Isothermal: heat is exchanged but $\\Delta T = 0$, so C is infinite.\n(d) Adiabatic: $\\Delta Q = 0$ while T changes, so C = 0.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-177","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":177,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"An ideal gas is in equilibrium in a rigid vessel of volume $0.0249\\ m^3$ at pressure $1.0 \\times 10^5$ Pa and temperature 300 K. A thin partition is now imagined to divide the vessel into two equal halves. How many moles of gas are there in each half? (Take $R = 8.3$ J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$.)","image":"","options":["1.0 mol","2.0 mol","0.25 mol","0.5 mol"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: P = $1.0\\times10^5$ Pa, V = 0.0249 m$^3$, T = 300 K, R = 8.3 J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$.\nFormula: $\\mu = \\frac{PV}{RT}$.\nSubstituting: $\\mu = \\frac{1.0\\times10^5 \\times 0.0249}{8.3\\times300} = \\frac{2490}{2490} = 1.0$ mol in total.\nThe amount of gas is extensive, so it is halved in each part. P and T, being intensive, stay unchanged.\nSo each half has 0.5 mol.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-178","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":178,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In a Carnot cycle with an ideal gas, the work done by the gas in the adiabatic expansion is exactly cancelled by the work done on the gas in the adiabatic compression.\nReason (R): The work done by an ideal gas in an adiabatic process is $\\frac{\\mu R(T_1 - T_2)}{\\gamma - 1}$, which depends only on the initial and final temperatures.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true: from NCERT Eq. 11.16, adiabatic work is $W = \\frac{\\mu R(T_1 - T_2)}{\\gamma - 1}$, which depends only on the end temperatures.\nA is true: step 2→3 goes from $T_1$ to $T_2$ and step 4→1 goes from $T_2$ to $T_1$. The two works are equal in magnitude and opposite in sign.\nThe cancellation happens precisely because the work depends only on the same temperature change, so R explains A.\nNet work therefore comes only from the two isotherms.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-THERMODYNAMICS-179","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Thermodynamics","qno":179,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Thermodynamics","type":"mcq","question":"Two moles of an ideal gas expand quasi-statically and isothermally at 300 K. The volume increases from 10 L to 20 L. How much heat is absorbed by the gas? (Take $R = 8.31$ J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$ and $\\ln 2 = 0.693$.)","image":"","options":["3456 J","4986 J","1728 J","1501 J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\mu = 2$, T = 300 K, $V_2/V_1 = 2$.\nFor an isothermal process of an ideal gas, $\\Delta U = 0$, so $Q = W = \\mu RT \\ln\\frac{V_2}{V_1}$.\nSubstituting: $Q = 2 \\times 8.31 \\times 300 \\times 0.693$.\nSo Q ≈ 3456 J.\nOption (2) omits the log term. Option (4) uses $\\log_{10}$ instead of the natural log.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 11 · Thermodynamics","difficulty":"medium"}]});
